    16	
    17	## Where we enter
    18	
    19	The switchback shelf above a valley with no name: the second day past the Line, the second camp beyond it and the first fire. Five people, a cart and a brown gelding with one white foot, three numbered boxes and Seln's locked case under one canvas; Hesk's satchel holds the current log, full old volume, observation notebook, and two no-return-address letters held for a westbound courier. Cael is eighteen, with nine held fragments, one uncounted anomaly, no deployment since the Line, no open injury, and a fading left-forearm bruise one week from gone. “Nothing anywhere was deciding what he was, except him.” [B6-reconciled 2026-10-11: audit A1, D1–D4, N1–N4, N7; coordinator O1, O2]
    20	
    21	## What this movement is for
    22	
    23	Deliver Arc 3's promise whole before anything complicates it: a country with roads and no paper, an institution (the board) that prices outcome and never tier, and a discipline (the hold) that turns the discipline of not using into the only competence that pays. [B6-reconciled 2026-10-11: audit A3] Teach the system hard. Turns and promises:
    24	
    25	- **The skip goes on the page, not into a summary log.** The log-voice can open the movement, but scenes carry it. Day 4, the ford: the westbound courier on a mule takes the two letters and does not ask what they are, and he writes down that she did not. Day 6, behind three carts: the boy's hand knocked down ("Not on the road."); "You're paper, aren't you" … "Then you know how to keep quiet. Keep it." The etiquette is learned as observed, not explained: walk quiet, carry steel, no fire you don't need, don't declare. Claim, evidence, ruling: hold.
    26	- **OWNER #23 / C1 (binding):** in Lowmarch's first week the horse and cart are sold. A horse is fodder and a sign that says *paper*; the scrub ruts past Lowmarch take mules. The sale and remaining Book 6 purse become the sealed reserve; the board purse alone is “in hand.” [B6-reconciled 2026-10-11: audit N7] Give it one good scene and an argument with a joke in it, then let it go.
    27	- **Lowmarch, day 9, and the board read like the Cinder House board.** Fourteen slips, not one pricing the crew. Pike: sixties, one arm, "Copper, Force, thirty years on the roads." "Walk, carry, climb, fight." / "In that order?" / "Depends on the day." A heading opened with five names and five columns: Job, Hirer, Witness, Outcome, Price. No tier column. He reads it upside down and looks for the missing column "the way you look for a stair in the dark, with a foot."
    28	- **Three contracts as short episodes.** The ferry escort (day 14; six crossings; two marks; standing where they can be seen, a job he has done before "at a higher price and for worse people"). The fence-line at the steading (days 22–24; Brom cuts six hours; Karis is asked not to light, does not ask why, and writes down the not-asking; "Cheap"). The wash (day 33; rope; Lira on the far bank, Brom on the near; three wheels; "You're not paper" / "I am"). The ferry broker may carry the hook-and-bar mark, as a plant only. Nothing about him is implied.
    29	- **The hold as a learning arc.** Lira trying runs that end with nothing (a burst is a shout); Brom carrying a load and not hardening; Karis keeping one point she will not light; Cael cataloguing the nine as things *held*, each with its rent: Storm: at Gold, air or body but not both; lane bill ten/ten/twelve, clean eleven/eleven/thirteen, with everything answering a beat late as bill, not flag, untested past three. Anchor: fixed points, lines and cage; one purposeful unbinding, one uncommanded twelve-second doorway hold; perception sure about ten feet, faint twelve, absent fourteen; reach/rate/price otherwise unknown. [B6-reconciled 2026-10-11: audit A4, A5] Shadow drifting at dusk (twice, on a road with nobody on it), Anchor wanting every doorway since the Ostrand road, Storm sitting heavy. **No fragment is used in this movement.** The log reads "Deployment since the Line: none" through day 49.
    30	- **The Thornwater road (days 41–43), ≈4,000:** the carter at the fire ("Two carts went quiet on it last month"); the stretch at dusk after a wheel cost an hour; positions; the valley's noise; **Lira's hand up at a hole in the noise**; Seln without turning, "Don't declare anything." / "Walk. Same pace."; "I could clear it." / "No." / "I know. I'm saying it so I've said it."; Brom becoming exactly as large as his body and no larger; Karis deciding five times not to take out the notebook; the five items (keeps station at forty-five yards; makes no sound; tracks the buckle; won't cross the gravel fan; reacts to nothing because they gave it nothing); it stops a hundred and ten yards short of the fence. No blow struck. [B6-reconciled 2026-10-11: audit A2; this line also keeps the generic "last month" per coordinator O4]
    31	- **Thornwater:** the gate, the woman with the lantern, the bowman who doesn't point; "First crew I've hired that knew to shut up."; "Stillhound… You shut up. It went home."; "You're paper." / "Yes." / "Hm."; the slip marked; the grain loft; six days; the carter back on the fourth; Pike's copied line; the log with "Note the date. Season's open, and for once it isn't theirs."
    32	- **Lowmarch, day 49 — the board anatomized:** a woman captain's line read before and after; the eleven-bale captain ("Say it again and I'll write it"); "The book's for me. The saying's for them."; the wall at the lower inn; "A Bronze who lost my carter…"; "Did you ever hear of a ledger-keeper called Vell?" / "No."; Seln's finger on the carter's map, the asset-restriction **said once and put away**, the map turned east. "Four lines. Nobody lost, nobody hurt, nobody knows you." Brom: "Eleven."
    33	- **The first pole, written plainly in the log** (protected).
    34	- **Teague across the sill:** he counts his three before the room. He reads Lira from her feet, Brom as a fact, Karis with the beginning of a smile, and Seln on the second pass. Cael reads him back, item by item. Neither speaks. The last of the day lies across the sheet: one word at the top, five names at the bottom, eleven lines of light between.
