     1	# Chapter 2 — Fourteen Slips
     2	
     3	They walked behind the three carts for two days more, and in all that time nobody spoke to them.
     4	
     5	It was not unfriendly, for Cael had been ignored by experts, in a house on a bluff, and he knew the feel of it; this was something else, and he came to think of it as being let alone. On the eighth evening the woman with the hides walked back along the road at dusk and laid a flat cake on the shaft of their cart, still warm from being carried inside her coat, and walked away again before anybody could thank her. Lira broke it in five, and Brom said it was barley, and nobody found anything to add.
     6	
     7	The country came down as they went. The red trees thinned and gave over to a broad pale tree with leaves that showed silver when the wind turned them, which Karis also declined to name, and the river they were following grew slow and wide and took in others. It was warm in the bottom. The dust stood behind the carts until long after they had passed, and there were flies about the gelding's eyes all day.
     8	
     9	On the ninth day, a little before noon, the road went up over a last low rise, and the old man at the front lifted his hand without turning round. It was the first sign he had made to them since the halting-ground.
    10	
    11	Cael came up to the crest beside the wheel and looked down at a town.
    12	
    13	It lay on the far side of a river a hundred yards across, green-brown and unhurried, and he read it from the water upward, as he had been taught to read a floor. There was a landing, with a flat boat at it. Above the landing stood a long low building with a deep eave, and on the rise behind that a taller one with two chimneys; between them a smithy was sending up the first smoke he had seen in three days. Along the river below the buildings ran a meadow, half a mile of it, with a single great ash standing in the middle and carts and tents scattered round the tree like washing.
    14	
    15	That was all there was, and he went on looking for the rest of it.
    16	
    17	He looked for the wall first. Every town he had ever entered had kept one, even Denvash, which was nothing, and a wall meant a gate, and a gate meant a man in it with a book. He looked for the station next, the low stone house with the windowless building behind, and for a board of hours, and for any roof with a seal burned into its sign. His eye went over the place three times in search of them, like a tongue going back to where a tooth has been.
    18	
    19	"There's no wall," he said.
    20	
    21	"There is no anything," said Seln. He had come up on the other side of the cart, and he was not looking at the buildings at all. He was looking at what left them: a road that went north along the water, and another that climbed east over a saddle, and a third going south into low hills. "I count four ways out, with ours, and I think there is a fifth that somebody does not much use. I should like to know where each of them goes."
    22	
    23	"Ask someone," said Lira.
    24	
    25	"I do not think one does, here."
    26	
    27	A cable ran over the river, bank to bank, sagging to the water in the middle. The old man had gone down to a post at the road's foot and was pulling on a cord, and after a while the flat boat on the far side began to move, though nobody was rowing it.
    28	
    29	It took them the better part of an hour to work out how. The boat was a scow, square at both ends, with a rail, and it hung on the cable by two running blocks; a second rope went from its bow to a round shed on the far bank, open at the sides, and inside that shed something walked in a circle. When it walked, a drum turned and the rope came in. When it stopped, which it did every few turns, a man's voice came across the water, patient and tired, and after a while it went on again.
    30	
    31	"That's a mule," said Brom. He had his hand over his eyes. "An old one. He's got a bad off hind, and he's being asked to do it anyway."
    32	
    33	"You can't tell that from here."
    34	
    35	"I can tell how he's putting it down."
    36	
    37	The carts went first, as was plainly their right, two to a crossing. A boy took a copper for each wheel and a copper for each beast, and he looked at the gelding as the courier had looked at it, and charged for it as a mule.
    38	
    39	Their own turn came last. The gelding had not cared for the Ost and did not care for this, and he went onto the planks sideways, with the whites of his eyes showing. Lira told him in a hoarse steady voice exactly what she thought of an animal that had forded three rivers on his own feet and now objected to being carried over a fourth. Halfway across the rope went slack. They hung there on the cable, turning a little, with the water sliding by underneath, until the tired voice on the bank had said its say and the drum began to creak again.
    40	
    41	---
    42	
    43	Nobody told them where to stand in the meadow, so they stood and watched where everyone else stood.
    44	
    45	The carts lay along the river side with their shafts down and a stone behind each wheel. The tents were farther in, in no order he could find, of hide and tarred canvas and old sail, and they were not the same tents from one hour to the next. While Brom was getting the gelding out of the shafts a crew of four struck one within thirty yards of him, rolled it, and walked out by the north road with it on a pole between two of them; and before the flattened grass had lifted, a woman with a handcart had pitched on the same place. He did not see anybody ask anybody's leave. He had counted seventeen tents from the ferry, out of habit, and there were twenty when he counted again; and by evening only nine of the first seventeen stood where they had stood at noon.
    46	
    47	The old man had gone straight to the ash. He drew his two mules into its shade as a man walks into his own kitchen, and nobody else stood within twenty feet of him, though the shade was the best in the meadow.
    48	
    49	"That's his," said Lira. "The tree. Look how they've left it for him."
    50	
    51	"Or he is only the first who thought of it, and the least pleasant to move," said Seln.
    52	
    53	"It can be both."
    54	
    55	It smelled of mules, and tar, and of something frying. A woman went between the tents with a board on her head and flat loaves stacked on the board; a man sat cross-legged in the grass with a harness across his knees, driving an awl through it with the heel of his hand. Three children and a yellow dog were trying to get a goat to go somewhere it had decided against. After nine days of ridges and a river, it seemed to Cael as loud as a city.
    56	
    57	There were fires here, and he saw it almost at once, and felt Karis see it beside him, for her pencil came out of the spine of her notebook. There were cook-fires at half the tents, and a woman frying something over one, and smoke lying along the river in a low blue shelf. People were talking, too, in ordinary voices, and somebody laughed aloud. Down by the smithy a man was holding the end of an iron bar across the anvil with his bare forearm, and the arm was grey to the elbow, and nobody round him so much as turned to look.
    58	
    59	"So it is the road," said Karis, very low, writing. "It isn't a rule about fire, or about speaking, or about a Path. It's a rule about doing any of them on a road. Here they do all three." She underlined something. "That's narrower than I had it. I don't know that it's any clearer."
    60	
    61	"Does it change the ruling?" said Brom.
    62	
    63	"No," said Cael. "It tells us where the ruling runs. We hold on a road. I'd hold here as well, until somebody's seen what we're like."
