    84	
    85	It was a ship's plank.
    86	
    87	Somebody had brought it up from some river long ago, twelve feet of oak as thick as his wrist, tarred black, and set it breast-high on two posts under the eave of the lower inn, where the weather could not get at it and the whole meadow could. Slips were tacked along it from one end to the other. They were on anything that would take a mark: bark, mostly, peeled thin and written on the pale side, and scraps of hide, and one cedar shingle, and a single piece of real paper that had been somebody's waybill and was written across the back. Each hung by one iron tack, and the oak round them was pitted with the holes of older tacks, hundreds of them, in drifts, so that he could see where the slips had clustered thickest in other years. It was a board that had been used hard for a long time by people who did not waste a nail.
    88	
    89	Lira went along it fast, with her finger out, and her face fell as she went.
    90	
    91	"There's no card," she said. "There's no bouts at all. Look at it. There isn't a single name on the whole thing."
    92	
    93	"There are fourteen slips."
    94	
    95	"I can count. What I mean is that nobody's *fighting* anybody."
    96	
    97	Cael did not answer her, because he had started at the left-hand post and was reading.
    98	
    99	He had done this once before. He had been new to a district, and not long past his Kindling, and he had stood in front of a board at a crossroads in Ardenmere for the better part of a morning before he let himself go near the yard it spoke of. A girl had told him afterward that almost nobody did that. Most people either never looked, or walked straight in and were carried out the same day, and he had done a third thing, which was to stand in the road and try to understand the board first. It had served him then, and he did it again now, slowly, a slip at a time.
   100	
   101	*Six crossings, the landing's goods; a crew to stand; two marks.* There was a seal on that one, pressed in dark wax.
   102	
   103	*Fence, the steading on the north water; posts cut and set; four marks and flour.*
   104	
   105	*The well on the south road; four.*
   106	
   107	*Shingles, the upper inn's lee side; one mark and board while it lasts.*
   108	
   109	*Fleece down from the high fold, eleven bales; a mark the bale, delivered dry.*
   110	
   111	*Strayed, one mule, grey, off the cold road; half its worth to who brings it.*
   112	
   113	There were eight more. One wanted a night-watch kept on the landing, and one wanted stones picked out of a field, and one was a message to be walked to a hold four days off, for a mark and feed. Most were in the same square black hand, as if one person had written them out for others; but beside the words on each somebody had drawn a little picture, a wheel or a fence or a boat with sticks standing in it, for the use of people who could make nothing of the words.
   114	
   115	"Eleven bales against one crew," said Lira, who had come back along the plank and was reading the fleece over his shoulder. "I'd back the bales. Is that all there is? Carrying wool down a hill?"
   116	
