   267	Karis had the field archive open, by the light. She had written five words across the head of a clean leaf, in her small exact hand, with a ruled line under them. Now she went back along the row and drew a second line beneath one of the five.
   268	
   269	"You've underlined it," said Lira.
   270	
   271	"*Outcome.* Yes."
   272	
   273	"Why that one?"
   274	
   275	"Because the other four are facts about a contract. Who wanted what done, and who saw it, and what was paid." Karis laid the pencil in the fold. "That one is a fact about us. It's the only place in his whole book where he writes down anything we did. I should like to know what words he uses."
   276	
   277	Cael wrote the day by the same light, on his knee.
   278	
   279	*Ninth day. Lowmarch. A river, a ferry, two inns, a smith, a meadow, and no wall. Fires and voices and Paths in the meadow; none of them on the road. A board with fourteen slips on it and not one that prices the crew. A heading in a one-armed man's book: five names, five columns, nothing under them. He asked how old I was. He asked nothing else.*
