     1	# Chapter 6 — Thornwater
     2	
     3	The woman who held the lantern kept it low, at her knee, where it would light the ground and not her own eyes.
     4	
     5	Cael noticed that before he noticed her. She was a broad grey-braided woman of fifty or sixty with her sleeves rolled to the elbow, and she stood in the gap of her gate and counted them through it as a farmer counts sheep at a hurdle: the dark coat first, then the two of them abreast, and the mule, and the cart, and last a very large young man with a hedging bill. She moved her lips for each. When the tailboard was inside she put her shoulder to the gate and shut it, and dropped a bar as thick as his arm.
     6	
     7	Then she let out her breath, and so did everybody else.
     8	
     9	There was a platform beside the gate, on the inner face of the fence, six feet up, with a ladder to it. A man stood there. He had a bow in his left hand and an arrow on the string, and Cael had seen enough people threaten him to know that this one was not doing it. The bow hung at the length of his arm. The arrow pointed at the planks between his boots. He was looking out over the top of the posts, down the valley, at something a hundred and ten yards off beside a white stone, and he did not once look round at the people who had just come in under him.
    10	
    11	Inside the fence there was noise. Cael had forgotten, in a mile, that there could be. A dog was barking at the mule, and a door stood open on a lit room, and somewhere a child was being told for the last time to come in.
    12	
    13	The carter took his hand off the mule's nose. He wiped the palm down his breeches, slowly, and turned to the five of them, in the lantern's light, with an expression that Cael could not at first place. It turned out to be respect, on a face that was not much used to showing it.
    14	
    15	"First crew I've hired that knew to shut up," he said.
