    33	Cael looked at Karis. She had the notebook out at last; it had come out of her coat the moment the bar dropped, as though it had been on a spring. She wrote one word at the head of a clean leaf, and looked at it, and drew a single line beneath it. Then she looked up at the woman with the lantern, and he saw the second question arrive in her face, whole, with its grammar. It was the question of what a thing like that could be waiting for.
    34	
    35	She shut her mouth on it. He was close enough to see what that cost her, and it was a good deal more than a pencil had.
    36	
    37	The woman had been looking at the cart. She lifted the edge of the small canvas and saw three roped boxes on top of her grain, with a paper tag on each. She let the canvas down. Then she raised the lantern a little, for the first time, and went over the five of them with it, their boots and their coats and the cut of Lira's hair, and stopped at Cael.
    38	
    39	"You're paper."
    40	
    41	"Yes."
    42	
    43	"Hm."
