    46	
    47	"Delivered at my fence," she said. "And nothing lost that I can see. I'll stand to that." She gave the slip back to the cart. "There's a loft over the barn. The grain goes up in the morning, and you can have what room it leaves. Ladder's sound."
    48	
    49	"We're obliged," said Brom.
    50	
    51	"You're hired. That's different. He'll not go down again till the sun's straight over, and I don't feed people for sitting."
    52	
    53	She went in with her lantern. The door shut on the lit room, and it was dark in the yard, and Cael looked up at the platform.
    54	
    55	The bowman had not moved. But as Cael watched he eased the string, and took the arrow off it, and slid it back into a case at his hip. Whatever he had been looking at, by a white stone, was not there any longer.
    56	
    57	Brom carried the boxes up the ladder by feel, one at a time, and Karis counted them from below in a voice that was not quite steady. The loft smelled of last year's barley. They lay down in it without lighting anything, and it was a long while before anybody slept.
    58	
    59	"Five things," said Cael, into the dark. "I counted five. And a number."
    60	
    61	"Tell me in order," said Karis. "I'll write them in the morning. I think that if I tried to hold a pencil now I should break it."
    62	
    63	---
    64	
    65	By daylight it was a hold like any other, he supposed, though it was the first he had been inside.
    66	
    67	The fence closed off the whole head of the valley and ran on round, up one shoulder and down the other, a quarter of a mile about. Within it stood a long house, and the barn with the loft over it, and a yard for carts, and a well with a sweep; there were a dozen people or so, of every age, and a line of geese that hissed at all of them alike. The grain was the point of the place. It came up from the river country in sacks, on one man's cart, and it sat under that roof until other carts came down from holds farther east to fetch it, and the woman with the grey braid stood between the two and saw that nobody was cheated in either direction.
    68	
    69	She ran it as Bracken had run a counter. Cael saw that within an hour, and it warmed him to her more than any welcome could have done. She had a tally of the sacks on a board by the barn door, in strokes, and when Brom took the first one up the ladder she drew her charcoal through a stroke without being asked.
    70	
    71	There were forty-one sacks, and each weighed about as much as Lira.
    72	
    73	Brom took them up one at a time, on his right shoulder, with his left hand for the ladder. It was the tree on the gravel bar over again, with a twelve-foot drop under it and a floor of flagstones, and Cael, who was drawing water at the well, watched every trip from the side of his eye. On the seventeenth, a rung went. It cracked through under the leading boot with a sound like the wheel had made, and the whole of Brom's weight and a sack of barley came down eighteen inches onto the rung beneath.
    74	
    75	He did not drop this load, for Lira was standing under him with the next one. Cael had his own breath stopped, and Anchor had already found the ladder's rail and asked whether it should be held.
    76	
    77	*Rail. Leave it.*
    78	
    79	Brom's left arm took it. The knuckles went white and the shoulder stood out through his shirt, and the ladder bowed like a drawn bow and came back; and when he had found the rung with his foot he went on up, more slowly, and set the sack down in the loft before he looked at his own hands. They were brown, both of them. Iron Skin had not come.
    80	
    81	"I didn't have time to choose," he said, at the foot of the ladder. He was a little pale. "It just wasn't there. I think that's the first one I didn't have to say no to."
    82	
    83	"What did you have instead?"
    84	
    85	"An arm." He flexed it. "It's enough, for a ladder. I'd not have known."
    86	
    87	The woman with the braid had seen the whole of it from the barn door. She said nothing to Brom. She went and found a boy, and sent him for a hammer and a billet of ash, and the rung was mended before the eighteenth sack went up.
    88	
    89	Lira ran the fence at first light, on the second morning.
    90	
    91	She had asked nobody. She went round the inside of the posts, the whole quarter mile, flat out, with the geese scattering, and Cael saw her come down the last straight toward the gate with her head back and her arms going. Three strides short of the bowman's platform she did what she had spent a month on a gravel bar failing to do. She stopped. She did it between one stride and the next, dead, on both feet, with nothing at the end of it but herself, breathing.
    92	
    93	The bowman had been watching her come. He was a lean, weathered man of no particular age, who seemed to live on that platform, and Cael had not yet heard him use his voice. He looked down at Lira standing under him with her hands on her knees. Then he lifted two fingers from the stave of his bow, an inch, and laid them back.
    94	
    95	Lira saw it. She went red to the ears and ran the whole fence again.
    96	
    97	"What does she stop like that for?" said the woman, at Cael's elbow. She had come out with a basin. "Like a cart with its wheels chocked."
    98	
    99	"She's learning to. It's harder than going on."
   100	
   101	"She was fast enough before she stopped." The woman threw her water at the geese. "Is that a thing they teach, where you're from?"
   102	
   103	"No. They teach the other. Every school west of the Line gives you a rank for declaring quicker than the next person, and none of them has a word for this." He watched Lira go round. "We call it the hold. We're having to find it out for ourselves."
   104	
   105	She gave him a long look, the same that she had given him over a lantern, and went in.
   106	
   107	Karis spent that day and the next with one leaf.
   108	
   109	She had made a table of it in her exact hand. At the head stood the word, underlined once, and under the word his five counts in the order he had given them: *keeps station at forty-five yards; makes no sound; follows the buckle with its head; will not cross the gravel fan; answers nothing, having been given nothing.* Beneath those stood the figure, *110*, and after it, in smaller writing, *of C.'s strides, gate to stone.* She had gone out to the well in the heat of noon and paced a hundred and ten of her own along the inside of the fence, which came to a good deal less, so as to know in her legs how far it was.
   110	
   111	"It stops short," she said. She was sitting on the top box with the leaf on her knee. "Short of what? I can think of four answers, and I've no way of choosing among them. It may stop short of the fence, or of the people behind the fence. It may be that something lies under the ground at that distance and nowhere else. Or a hundred and ten is simply as near as it ever comes to anything, and the stone is an accident."
   112	
   113	"Which do you think?"
   114	
   115	"I don't. I've a noun and six numbers." She looked at the page with an exasperation that was very nearly affection. "I haven't a single verb. I don't know one thing that it *does*."
   116	
   117	---
   118	
   119	Seln went out by the gate at noon on the forty-fifth day, when the sun stood straight over the valley and there was no shadow under the posts.
   120	
   121	He had waited for the hour, and he went without his Path, as he had gone everywhere since a crossroads fire; Shadow was a declaration like any other, and he had told four people to make none. Nobody had given him the reason for the hour, and he had not sought it; but a carter of twenty-two years kept that rule, and so did a hold that had stood behind this fence for longer, and he had not lived to his age by supposing himself wiser than the people of a place. The man on the platform watched him lift the bar. He did not call down. Seln had the feeling of being entered in a column, correctly, by somebody who would not trouble him about it afterward.
   122	
   123	He walked to the white stone. It was a hundred and four of his paces; the boy's stride was a little shorter than his own.
   124	
   125	The ground beside it was soft, and it had kept everything.
   126	
   127	There were four prints, set square, where a thing had stood. Each was as broad as his palm and deeper at the toe than at the heel, and that depth told him how long. A creature that stands a moment leaves a mark like a seal in wax. One that stands until its weight has settled forward leaves a mark like these, and he judged that it had been there from the time a gate opened until the time an arrow went back into its case.
   128	
   129	It had not circled or lain down. He could find no place where it had shifted its feet. When it went, it had turned in its own length, and gone back down the valley on the line it had come by, at a walk; he followed that line for two hundred yards with the grass over his head, and the prints lay as evenly as stitches. He cut across to the ruts at three places and paced the gap. It was forty-five yards at each, as nearly as his legs could tell him.
   130	
   131	He stood in the grass for some time with that.
   132	
   133	He knew what he was looking at, and it was not anything that he knew about animals, of which he knew very little. It was his own trade. A man set to follow another is given his distance. He is told to keep it, and neither close nor fall back; and it is chosen so that the ordinary noise of the person in front will cover him, and so that one particular thing, if it should happen, will reach him across the gap. He does not act until it does. He may walk behind a subject for a year and never be given leave.
   134	
   135	Seln had stood at that distance outside a great many doors. He had stood there for two years below a bluff, in effect, with a clerk's pen in his hand.
   136	
   137	*Subject animal kept station on the party at forty-five yards for something over half a mile, without approach. Halted one hundred and ten yards from the hold's gate and remained until the gate was barred. Withdrew at a walk by the line of its coming. It was waiting upon a signal. The signal was not given. This officer does not know what the signal is.*
   138	
   139	That was the form, and it had made itself without his leave, as it did. He read the last sentence over. He had written *nothing further to report* at the foot of seven hundred returns, and he had never before written that he did not know a thing. It had always been possible to leave such matters out.
   140	
   141	There were three ways out of Thornwater. He had known that from the loft door on the first morning, and he had taken them one at a time, at this hour only.
   142	
   143	The first was the road they had come by. The second went north from a wicket in the fence, up to grazing, and ended at a stone hut with nobody in it. The third left by a gate on the eastern side and went on up the valley, over a low place in the hills. He had walked two hours along that one, yesterday, with the sun on his head. It was a road like the others. It had ruts, and the ruts had been used, though not lately, and nobody had told him that it was different from the rest and nobody had told him that it was the same. He turned back at the two hours because he had promised himself that he would be within a fence, any fence, well before the light began to go.
   144	
   145	He had now eight roads in his head, and the manner of an animal's waiting. It was the fullest return he had ever composed.
   146	
   147	He considered, walking back up the ruts to the gate, whether any part of it bore upon the day in hand. That was the rule he had made for himself, and he applied it with care, since he distrusted rules that he found convenient. They were inside a fence. They would go down this valley again behind a cart, at noon, with the sun over them. On that day there was nothing in his eight roads that five people needed in order to put one foot before the other, and so he would keep it, as he had kept the rest.
   148	
   149	It would bear, some day. He was nearly sure of that. Somebody would be going to this stretch at the wrong hour and for a reason, and then he would lay the whole of it out on a table without being asked, down to the depth of a toe-print.
   150	
   151	In the meantime he walked about with it, and listened for the level voice. *What did you see?* The woman of the hold did not ask it. She had watched him go out at noon and she would watch him come in, and she plainly thought that a man's errands at that stone were his own. The archer did not ask. The boy looked at the dust on his boots each evening, and at the direction it had come from, and wrote down, Seln was fairly certain, only that there had been dust.
   152	
   153	He put his hand on the bar of the gate. Above him the man on the platform shifted his weight, and said nothing, and went on looking down the valley.
   154	
   155	Seln found that he liked him very much, and went in to carry sacks.
   156	
   157	---
   158	
   159	The carter was back on the fourth day, an hour past noon, with the mule in a lather.
   160	
   161	He had gone out at noon on the first full day of their stay, with the witnessed slip in his hat and nothing in the cart, sitting on the tailboard as he had not once sat while the grain was on it; and an empty cart behind a rested mule, it seemed, could do in one day what a loaded one did in two. He had brought salt up with him, and a keg of nails, and a bolt of blue cloth that three women came out of the house to look at.
   162	
   163	He had also brought a scrap of bark. He took it out of his hat and held it out to Cael between two fingers, as though he were not sure that he ought to be touching it.
   164	
   165	"From the one-armed man. He's wrote it in the book, and that's it copied. He says you're to read it, and if it's wrong you can tell him so when you're down, and he'll say it over for the meadow after." The carter rubbed his neck. "He never sends silver up a road. It's in his box."
   166	
   167	It was in the square black hand. Cael read it standing in the yard, with the other four at his shoulders.
   168	
   169	*Thornwater road, the stretch. Walked ahead of one cart, one carter, one mule. Delivered at the fence. No losses. Nine marks, paid.*
   170	
   171	"He's counted the mule," said Brom.
   172	
   173	"He would. It was in front of you the whole way."
   174	
   175	"I'm not complaining. I'd have counted it myself."
   176	
   177	Lira read it twice with her lips moving, and then looked up.
   178	
   179	"Is it wrong?"
   180	
   181	Cael went through it once more, a word at a time, as he would have gone through an instrument. There had been one cart. They had walked ahead of it. Everything that had gone in at the mouth of that valley had come to a fence. He could not find a word in it that he would have changed, and what struck him harder was that he could not find one that said anything about him at all, beyond what he had done on a particular evening.
   182	
   183	"No," he said. "I think it's the first thing anybody ever wrote about me that I'd have put my name to."
   184	
   185	"*No losses,*" said Karis. She had it already, on her own leaf. "On the ferry he wrote *nothing lost*. That was casks. Here the noun has changed, and it's because of what there was to lose. He is very exact. I wonder whether he knows how exact he is."
   186	
   187	The carter had been looking at his off wheel while they read.
   188	
   189	It had held, all the way down the valley and all the way back, and it was an ugly piece of work: a thorn peg for a pin, and a rim wound round with wire like a mended pot. Brom had been looking at it as well. He had looked at it every time he crossed the yard for three days, with the expression of a man who has left a knot tied wrong.
   190	
   191	"I'd like to do that properly," he said. "If there's seasoned ash on the place, and an iron pin."
   192	
   193	There were both. They did it that afternoon in the cart-yard, the two of them, with the wheel off and laid across a pair of trestles. Brom cut the split timber out of the rim and shaped a new one to the curve with a drawknife, taking a shaving at a time and trying it and taking another, and the carter held what he was told to hold. It took them until the shadows were long. When the wheel was on again Brom spun it, and stood with his head bent, listening; it ran round four full turns and never ticked once.
   194	
   195	The carter looked at the wheel for a while. Then he went to the front of his cart, and reached in under the seat board, and came back with a roll of something pale tied round with a thong.
   196	
   197	"I don't pay silver for a wheel," he said. "I'd not insult you. That's a thing I've got that you haven't."
   198	
   199	It was a calfskin, scraped thin and gone the colour of cream. Karis had it out of Brom's hands before he had finished untying it. She unrolled it on the tailboard, and held the corners down with her spread fingers, and for a moment did not breathe.
   200	
   201	It was a map.
   202	
   203	It had been drawn in oak-gall ink and gone over, here and there, with a burnt stick. There were roads on it, each a double line, as a wheel sees a road. Three wavy strokes stood for a ford and a square with a dot in it for a hold. A ring with a bar through it lay on a river in the west, and Cael knew the ferry. Between one square and the next ran rows of small ticks, and he had counted the ticks from the ring to a square at the head of a valley before he understood that they were days. Roads went off the hide's edge to the north, and the south, and the east. Wherever a wheel had no business, he had put down nothing at all, and the skin was bare.
   204	
   205	"That's twenty-two years," said the carter. He sounded almost embarrassed. "I've the same in my head, so I'll not miss it. You've been going about with a clever girl and three boxes of paper and not a road among you."
   206	
   207	"I shall keep it dry," said Karis. Her voice had gone very small. "I shall keep it flat, and dry, and I shall not let anybody put a cup on it."
   208	
   209	"It's a hide. You can put what you like on it." He scratched his jaw. "Roads are dry now, right through. That's the season open. There'll be work on every one of those lines from this week till the snow shuts the high ones."
   210	
   211	He went to see to his mule.
   212	
   213	---
   214	
   215	Cael wrote in the loft door that evening, with his legs hanging over the yard and the last of the sun on the page.
   216	
   217	He had not written much at Thornwater. The first night he had not trusted his hand, and since then he had drawn water, and carried what Brom could not, and helped Karis to pace out a number along a fence. He was eighteen, and for three days he had been a hired man on a grain hold, and it had suited him better than he would have guessed. But there was a scrap of bark in the pocket of Hesk's book now, where two letters had lain, and it seemed to him that the book ought to know.
   218	
   219	*Forty-sixth day past the Line. Thornwater: a grain hold at the head of a valley, two days east of the board. Fourth line, in Pike's hand, copied and brought up by the carter. Nine marks, in his box. Earned, eighteen. In hand tonight, a few coppers and a map.*
   220	
   221	*On the forty-third, at dusk, a thing the hold calls a stillhound went beside us for the last half of a mile. Five counts and a distance, given to Karis; she has the name and I have the numbers and neither of us has a reason. Nobody declared anything. Nobody drew. I put all nine down at every stride and they kept getting up.*
   222	
   223	*Deployment since the Line: none.*
   224	
   225	He stopped there and looked out over the fence.
   226	
   227	The carter had said it as he might have said that the hay was in. *That's the season open.* He had meant the roads, and the weather on them, and a board in a meadow with slips enough on it for every crew that could walk. He could not have known what the word had been to the person he said it to.
   228	
   229	Cael had lived by seasons since he was fourteen, and he had not owned one of them. The first had been the registry's, a round of assessments in a district office, and he had been carried through it like a parcel that nobody could find the right shelf for. After that it had been a circuit's, and a tournament's, with a bracket drawn by other people. Then it was a house's, an academic year, with a bell in it; and the last had been a seat's, under a hood, fourteen sittings long. Each of them had opened on a day that somebody else appointed. Each had closed with a paper.
   230	
   231	This one had opened because the mud had dried.
   232	
   233	*The carter says the roads are dry and the season's open: work on every line of his map from now until the snow. I have been in somebody's season since my Kindling. The registry had one, and the circuit, and the house on the bluff, and the seat at Ostrand. I never chose the day any of them began, and each one ended with a ruling about me.*
   234	
   235	*This one belongs to a one-armed man's book, and the weather. It has four lines in it. I can read all four.*
   236	
   237	*Note the date. Season's open, and for once it isn't theirs.*
   238	
   239	He read the last line over, and shut the pencil in the book.
   240	
   241	Down in the yard Brom was spinning a wheel again, for the pleasure of hearing nothing, with a row of geese drawn up behind him like a jury. Lira sat on the well's coping, binding the hilt of her knife. On the platform by the gate the bowman stood as he always stood, with his bow at the length of his arm, looking down the valley at a white stone.
   242	
   243	Cael looked where he was looking. There was nothing beside the stone, and the light was going. He buckled the book into the satchel and climbed down the ladder, to draw the evening's water before it was dark.

