     1	# Chapter 7 — Thirteenth
     2	
     3	They stayed six days at Thornwater, and went down on the forty-eighth morning in front of an empty cart.
     4	
     5	The carter was going back for his next load, and the boxes rode with him again, on a bed of folded sacks, since nobody had been asked to pay for them the first time. He took the stretch at noon. Cael walked the mile of it with the sun on the crown of his head, in the left-hand rut, with his knife on his belt and all nine of his hands held down; and he saw grass, and a split cart-shaft, and a white stone with nothing beside it. The frogs creaked from one end of the valley to the other and did not stop anywhere.
     6	
     7	They came over the saddle above Lowmarch in the afternoon of the forty-ninth day, and he knew before they were off the hill that the meadow had changed.
     8	
     9	There were twice the tents, for the dry roads had done it, as the carter had said they would: crews had come in from every quarter while the five were away, and the grass along the river was trodden brown, and three fires burned where there had been one. A knot of people stood at the plank's near end, under the long eave, as people stand at a counter when a clerk is giving out results.
    10	
    11	Pike had the book open and the stone on it. He looked up as they came along the eave, and looked again, and put his finger on a leaf without having to turn to it.
    12	
