    28	
    29	A woman was next. She was lean and burnt, with a strip of red cloth tied round her hair, and two men and a girl stood at her back; she laid a slip on the plank and stepped away from it with her arms folded. Pike read it to himself, and read it again, and then he gave it to the meadow.
    30	
    31	"North road. Four carts of salt and iron walked to the upper holds. Delivered at the last gate. One mule lamed. Fourteen marks."
    32	
    33	"Lame when I took him," said the woman.
    34	
    35	She had not raised her voice either. Pike looked at her, in his careful double fashion.
    36	
    37	"Which foot?"
    38	
    39	"Near fore. He was pointing it in the yard before a pack went on. His own carter will tell you."
    40	
    41	Pike lifted his head and called a name across the grass, and a man at the second fire stood up with a bowl in his hand. He was asked one question, and he answered it with a nod and sat down again.
    42	
    43	"One mule lame at taking," said Pike, and dipped his pen.
