    46	
    47	"Why twice?" said Lira.
    48	
    49	She had held it in since the fourteenth day, and Cael supposed that five weeks was a fair trial of anybody. Pike did not seem to mind, and went on counting silver for the woman with the red cloth.
    50	
    51	"Once before it's in, so she can say it's wrong. Once after, so it's said."
    52	
    53	"Said to who? It's written. It's in your book."
    54	
    55	Pike looked past her, down the length of the meadow. There were perhaps two hundred people in it that afternoon, cooking and mending and arguing over mules, and Cael watched him consider them.
    56	
    57	"Half this meadow can't read. The book's for me. The saying's for them."
    58	
    59	He pushed the last coin across.
    60	
    61	Cael had been written in a good many books. There was one in a district office with a single word against his name, and one that a mild man had filled for two years, and one on a shelf somewhere with slips in its pocket that no officer would own; and the thing they had all had in common was that they were shut. People had read them about him in rooms he was not let into. He had stood on a carved number for fourteen sittings and never once been shown the page.
    62	
    63	This book lay open on a plank in the weather, and its keeper stood up and shouted it at anybody who was frying dough.
    64	
    65	---
    66	
    67	The man after her was a big one, in a new coat, and he was pleased with himself.
    68	
    69	He had brought fleece down from the high fold. Cael remembered the slip, for it had hung on the plank the day they came: eleven bales, at a mark the bale, delivered dry. The big man laid it down with a slap. Somebody at the landing had put a row of strokes on it for a witness, eleven of them, and through the last a short bar had been drawn.
    70	
    71	Pike read it twice, and then he gave it out.
    72	
    73	"Fleece, down from the high fold. Eleven bales carried. Ten delivered dry, and one wet. Ten marks."
    74	
    75	"Eleven," said the big man.
    76	
    77	"Ten dry."
    78	
    79	"It rained on us the last mile. You can't call that the carrying." He had a good loud comfortable voice, and he looked round at the people by the plank as he used it, inviting them in. "Eleven bales went up on my mules and eleven came down to the landing. Eleven bales, eleven marks. That's the slip."
    80	
    81	Pike had the pen in his hand. He did not put it down, and he did not dip it. He looked at the big man once, and then a second time, and spoke quite mildly.
    82	
    83	"Say it again and I'll write it."
    84	
    85	Nobody at the plank moved.
    86	
    87	Cael did not understand it for the space of a breath, and then he did, and the hair stood on his arms. It was no threat, for Pike would write it, exactly, as he wrote everything. He would put down that a captain said eleven dry, and beside it that a witness at the landing had marked ten; and then he would stand up and say both to two hundred people, and say them again when the ink was on. It would be in the book for as long as there was a book. Every hirer who ever asked about that crew would be told that its captain had once stood at this plank and argued with a mark.
    88	
    89	The big man had got there as well. His eyes went over the faces, and the fires, and came back to the pen.
    90	
    91	"Ten," he said.
    92	
    93	"Ten delivered dry, and one wet. Ten marks." Pike wrote, and said it over, and paid, and the man in the new coat went off down the meadow with his neck red.
    94	
    95	"He'd only have written what was said," Lira whispered. "That's all he was going to do."
    96	
    97	"I know."
    98	
    99	"That's nothing. Anybody can write what somebody said."
