   101	"I've known a great many clerks," said Cael, "and I can think of two who would have."
   102	
   103	The plank was nearly full. The dry weather had brought slips in from everywhere, and there was hardly a hand's breadth of bare oak between the posts; he read along it by habit, the bark and the hide and the pictures drawn for people who had no letters. At the right-hand post his eye stopped.
   104	
   105	It was a piece of real paper, the first he had seen there since the waybill. There was a mark at its foot that he had watched a woman make with a charred stick, at a gate, by lantern light: a thorn twig, three spines to a side, and a wavy line under it.
   106	
   107	*Thornwater ford. Clearance. Forty marks.*
