   109	Under that, in the square black hand, in capitals, stood a word that was on no other slip, and after it a name.
   110	
   111	*HOLD. Teague.*
   112	
   113	"Forty," said Lira, beside him. Her voice cracked on it. "Forty *marks*. That's more than twice everything we've got."
   114	
   115	"Clearance of what?" said Brom.
   116	
   117	"I know of what," said Karis. "I have the word for it underlined."
   118	
   119	Cael said nothing, for he knew the road that ford was on, having walked it twice now, at a mule's pace, with his mouth shut.
   120	
   121	"What's HOLD?" said Lira, across the plank.
   122	
   123	"It means it's spoken for." Pike did not look up. "He's asked me to keep it by till he's back, and I've told him I would."
   124	
   125	"Who has?"
   126	
   127	Pike reached out with the feather end of his pen and touched the name.
   128	
   129	"Top of the sheet."
   130	
   131	"Can anybody ask for that?"
   132	
   133	"Anybody can ask." He blotted a line. "I've said yes to one."
   134	
   135	"And when's he back? It's only that it says forty."
   136	
   137	"He's not gone yet. He goes out at first light, up the cold road, and that's a week's work or near it. Then the ford's his." Pike considered the slip, and something crossed his face that Cael could not read. "It'll keep a week. Carters know the hour for that road."
   138	
   139	Lira was frowning at the name, and Cael knew the frown, since he had seen it turned on every card she had ever stood under.
