     1	# Chapter 56 — Doors
     2	
     3	The house kept no rest day in its last week, and the closing seminars sat through the Seventh-day as they did every year.
     4	
     5	Lira's was at the third bell, on the upper passage of the lecture range. *Panels and formats, the third year: revision.* It stood on her schedule in the program clerk's hand, between a drill she had already done and a meal she would not be eating in the refectory.
     6	
     7	"You've signed," said Cael, on the stair. "It's dry by now. You needn't."
     8	
     9	"I know I needn't." She had taken the tape off her wrists and put on a clean shirt. "That's why."
    10	
    11	He walked her to the door, because he had nothing else on the hill to do, and it was a strange thing to find out about himself at the third bell of a working day. For two years he had always been due somewhere: at a trestle in wing three, or a table at the top of the library, or the north wall at the changing of the coats. Now his name stood in a book with five words against it, and nobody on the bluff was expecting him anywhere.
    12	
    13	She went in with the others. There were eleven of them, third-years mostly, with their notebooks under their arms, and two of them looked at her and then quickly at each other, so that he knew the hall's news had gone round the refectory already. She took the seat she always took, at the end of the second row, by the wall. The door swung to behind the last of them and did not latch.
    14	
    15	He sat down on the bench in the passage. It was a hard oak bench under a window, where enrollees waited to be told a mark, and he had never once sat on it; from it he could see the court, and a slice of the room through the gap of the door, with the instructor's desk in it, and a board, and Lira's left shoulder.
    16	
    17	The instructor was a spare grey woman with chalk on both cuffs, who had sat on district panels for twenty years before she taught, and who spoke as she must have scored, without ornament.
    18	
    19	"Control," she said. "We finish where we began. You have all been told that a panel marks control on the landing, and that is not so. A panel marks what it can see of the landing from where it has been put, and it has generally been put on the east side, out of the sun. So you will be marked on the half of you that faces a table. Write that down, and then write down what follows from it."
    20	
    21	Chalk went on the board, and he watched Lira's shoulder, and it moved. She had her carter's book open on the desk's slope, the stitched one in oilcloth, with the pencil out of its spine, and she was writing.
    22	
    23	She wrote quickly. The instructor went on, about a fighter who chose her foot late and a table that would read the choosing as a fault, and how many exchanges it took a good panel to learn otherwise, and Lira turned a leaf. She asked one question, which he could not hear, and was answered at some length, and wrote the answer down as well.
    24	
    25	There was a clock at the passage's end, and he counted by it: five minutes, and then ten.
    26	
    27	The clock's hand came up to the quarter and went by, and Lira shut the book.
    28	
    29	He saw her shoulder go still, and then he saw all of her, because she had stood up. The chalk stopped, and eleven heads came round. She put the pencil back into the book's spine, and took her time over it.
    30	
    31	"Thank you. It's been a good class and you're a good teacher," said Lira.
    32	
    33	Nobody spoke. The grey woman stood with the chalk raised and looked at her across two rows of desks. She had marked a great many fighters in her time, and she marked this one, and whatever she entered, she did not say it aloud.
    34	
    35	"Leave the door open as you go, would you," said the instructor. "It's close in here."
    36	
    37	Lira nodded. She came down the side of the room past the others, with the book under her arm, and pushed the door wide, and left it so.
    38	
