    40	
    41	She had thought it would feel like Fenmark.
    42	
    43	That was what she had been braced for, all the way up the stair. It was a room with a board in it and a row of backs, and it had a door, and she had expected the old cold to come up through her boots, the cold that had lived in her knees since she was a girl. She had stood in a doorway there with a paper in her hand, filled in correctly, in somebody else's writing, and been put on the outside of that door by people who were being perfectly polite.
    44	
    45	It had not come. She stood in the passage with her back to the open door and waited for it, as you wait for a foot to tell you it has been hurt, and behind her the chalk had started again.
    46	
    47	The notes were real, and that had surprised her a little. She had meant to sit a quarter of an hour, for the form of it, and say her sentence. Then the grey woman had begun on what a table could see and what it couldn't, and Lira had found the pencil in her hand, because it was all true and nobody had ever said it to her so plainly. *A panel sees half of you.* She had turned to the back of the carter's book, under *ME*, and taken it down as fast as she could write.
    48	
    49	She thought it would be a good thing to know on a road. Somebody would have to stand on her east side out there and tell her what the landing looked like, and she thought she knew who, since he counted everything else.
    50	
    51	She had given the key back at dawn.
    52	
    53	She had drilled first, in the grey, with her boots off: front foot and back foot and the shoulders, and go on the fourth, and come down on whichever foot she liked. She had gone up and down the painted floor until the light came through the gallery shutters and showed her the crosses, and then she had put her boots on and sat on the bench and waited for him.
    54	
    55	Rooke came in at the first bell with his board under his arm, as he did every morning of his life, and stopped inside the door. He had looked at her on the bench, and at the key on her knee, and she had seen him understand it; he had very likely understood it at midnight, chalking a board in the dark.
    56	
