    69	She had stood over the book a long time. It was not praise, and nobody but a coach would have known what it meant. He had watched her ask her mended foot for leave, landing after landing, and he had written down the fault in one word; then he had watched her stop asking, and he had struck the word out. He had not written *cured*, or *sound*. He had written whose foot it was.
    70	
    71	"It is," she had said, which was his own answer, given back.
    72	
    73	He had grunted, and that was all. She had laid the key on the open page, on top of the word, and gone out past him into the court, and she had not looked round, for she thought he would not have wanted her to.
    74	
    75	And now she stood in a passage with a door open behind her.
    76	
    77	It was the same thing. She could see that plainly, and she was not going to tell herself a kinder story. It was a school, and a room with a board, and a girl going out of it for good with her certificate in her coat, and anybody watching from the court would have seen exactly what a porter at Fenmark had seen.
    78	
    79	But nobody had held the door for her this time, and nobody had filled in the line. She had written it herself, in capitals, an hour ago, and a clerk in a district office was going to have to copy it out.
    80	
    81	Cael had got up off the bench. He stood there with his bound arm held against his chest and that look on him, the one he wore when he was adding, and he did not ask her anything. He never did, until she had finished a step.
