     1	# Chapter 58 — Pending Jurisdiction
     2	
     3	The machine took a week over it, and did nothing that was not correct.
     4	
     5	The district officer's envelope came back to the seat at Ostrand on the Seventh-day evening, by his own hand, opened and endorsed. *No such enrollee; withdrawn this day; see the registrar's certificate.* The certificate followed in the house's bag on the First-day morning, with four leaves of the registry's own form under the house's seal, and one of the four had three lines of longhand in its margin.
     6	
     7	The seat's clerk read the margin twice. He had no heading for a margin, and he entered the leaf as received.
     8	
     9	He then had before him a ruling, given under the Compact's hand two days earlier, and an enrollment which that ruling revoked from the year's close; and the enrollment had ended of itself before the close arrived. He turned to the code. It had a word for a matter that has lost its subject, and he wrote the word in the proceedings' last column, small, and blotted it.
    10	
    11	*Moot.*
    12	
    13	It did not undo the ruling, for nothing undid a ruling. It meant only that the paper would lie in its press, complete and lawful, with nobody left under it.
    14	
    15	There remained the order for re-evaluation, and that was not the clerk's to close. It had a date upon it, and a room.
    16	
    17	So on the Fourth-day, which was the last of the academic year, a stout fair man unlocked a frame-house at the hour appointed, and took the cover off the frame and folded it. The president of the evaluation seat came, since the ruling had charged her to see to the conduct of the thing, and she was a woman who saw to what she was charged with. She brought her four evaluators. They stood on the flags in their plain coats, in a windowless room, with the plate dark at their feet.
    18	
    19	The order was called, and the name was read as the instruments had it.
    20	
    21	Nobody answered, and no house stood by to produce anybody. The president let the full hour run by the district's clock, as the code required. She kept her feet for the whole of it, and opened her mouth only to ask the time. At the hour's end she directed the junior chair to enter what had occurred.
    22	
    23	The code gave him his phrase. An order was executed when its hour had been kept and its room opened, and this one had been executed, in every particular, against nobody. He wrote *Re-evaluation: not executed; evaluand not produced*, which was how the form said the same thing. The president read the line and initialled it. Then the officer put the cover back over the frame and locked the door.
    24	
    25	That left the fourteenth chapter.
    26	
    27	It was to have applied in its order, upon a result, and there was no result. A frame that nobody has stood in returns nothing at all, and the first part and the second fell away for want of it.
    28	
    29	The ninth part was drafted more widely. It allowed the registry to restrict, freeze or hold whatever attached to an unclassified person, pending the resolution of his status, and it did not wait upon a frame. The fences for it had been walked in the first term, and five drawers in five offices held their lists. The instrument was sealed, and went out by five bags.
    30	
    31	It came back five times.
    32	
    33	The house on the bluff answered first, in a registrar's upright hand. No enrollment stood, and no sponsorship; no floor hours were held, and no travel paper had been issued under the house's seal that had not lapsed with the form. The district seat answered that it kept nothing in that name but the file. The archive at Ternhall returned that the privileges in question had been surrendered at a counter, by their holder, before the instrument was dated. A provincial registry returned that the practitioner named as an associate stood at Copper, as he had for years; it added that a finding in his favour lay unsigned, and that the registry could not restrict a rank which nobody had taken up.
    34	
    35	Each answer was true. Each office had looked for something to lay a hand on, and each had written down, in good faith, that it found nothing there.
    36	
    37	The fifth bag went to the far end of the country.
    38	
    39	Two polite men had been through Denvash before. They had come in the first term to ask after an instrument-maker's trade licences and the title to his shop, and they had come again in the recess to see whether his answer had changed, and it had not. They came now a third time, with the sealed instrument in a wallet, and asked the old man at the bench whether his grandson had left any property on the premises.
    40	
    41	He said that the boy had left a great many shavings, and that they were welcome to them.
    42	
    43	They looked at the title, which had one name on it, and at the licences. One of them, while the other wrote, took up a brass caliper from the tray by the window and tried its jaws, and asked what it cost. He was told. It was the price on the ticket, which was a third more than such a thing had cost the year before, for brass had gone up, and he paid it. The old man wrote him a receipt and wrapped the caliper in a twist of paper.
    44	
    45	The return said so. *No interest of the subject's found. One purchase made by the attending officer on his own account: a brass caliper, at the marked price. Receipted.* It was included for completeness.
    46	
    47	So the instrument lay on a desk with its five answers pinned to it, and the clerk who had charge of it turned to the last question on his form, which asked where the subject was.
    48	
    49	He was not anywhere. A watcher's line had him on the east road, four days out, with a cart, and there was no line after that. Beyond the last station no district ran, and a restriction must run in a district; it wanted a counter to be served at, and a book to be written in. The code had foreseen that, as it foresaw most things. The clerk found the phrase in a schedule and wrote it at the instrument's foot.
    50	
    51	*Asset-restriction: pending jurisdiction.*
    52	
    53	He put it in a drawer. It would lie there, sealed and entire, for as long as it must, and on the day that any one of four names was written in a book inside a district, it would be taken out and served within the hour.
    54	
    55	In the week after the close a junior clerk went up a bluff road with a stamp. He wrote in the right-hand column of a charter folder that the respondent institution had been found compliant. The chancellor gave him tea, and he said afterward that she had been perfectly civil.
    56	
    57	---
    58	
    59	The summary came to the Archmarshal's desk in the ordinary bag.
    60	
    61	It was the third thing from the top, and he took it in its turn. It was a single leaf of registry stock under the seal of the seat at Ostrand, and he knew the form. When a proceeding closed, its seat sent a summary to every office that had been named in it, and each office returned the leaf with whatever it wished to have stand beside the record. His office had been named twice: once as the author of an advisory, three sentences long, which the petition had quoted with approval, and again as the certifying office, which had been asked for its name and had declined to give it.
    62	
    63	There was a broad margin down the leaf's right side, ruled, and headed *Observations of the office.*
    64	
    65	He had filled that margin on some hundreds of summaries. It was a thing he was known for, and juniors were shown his annotations in their second year, as examples of how much could be said in a space two fingers wide.
    66	
    67	He read the leaf from its head to its seal.
    68	
    69	*In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward. Enrollment: closed by resignation; the house's certificate annexed. Proceedings: moot. Re-evaluation: not executed. Asset-restriction: pending jurisdiction.*
    70	
    71	There were four lines, and he could find nothing wrong with any of them.
    72	
    73	He laid his left hand on the blotter. It did not like mornings. Below the window the porter was feeding the pigeons from his paper bag, and they had come to him in their half-circle.
    74	
    75	*Closed by resignation.* He turned that one over longest. He had seen four young people and an older man in a room with nothing of anybody's in it, and had read what they brought him, and had told them to their faces what he would not do. He had not asked what they would do. It had not been his to ask, and he had thought it better not to carry the answer. He saw it now, in a clerk's round hand: they had not appealed, and they had not published; they had waited for the one door in the code that left a house clean, and they had walked out of it in good order, with a registrar's certificate behind them.
    76	
    77	*Pending jurisdiction.* So they had gone where no district ran.
    78	
    79	He was glad of it. It was not a thing to write in a margin, and he noted it only in passing, as he would have noted a reading that came out a hair above tolerance.
    80	
    81	He took up his pen.
    82	
    83	There were true things he might have set there. He considered each of them, as he considered everything, and with each he saw at once what would be done with it. He had written three true sentences about this practitioner before, in an advisory, as exactly as he could make them, and somebody had lifted them out and used them as a warrant. He had petitioned to be heard and been refused in two lines. He had written a finding by a candle and addressed it to nobody; it was in the lowest drawer, under a tab, and no proceeding had ever come that was worth sending it to. The one sentence of his that they still needed he had withheld, and a clerk had entered its absence on a form.
    84	
    85	He had forty years' practice in saying what was so, and he had learned in this one year that a true sentence, once it left his desk, belonged to whoever picked it up.
    86	
    87	*The instrument did not fail for want of care.* That was true, and they would cite it.
    88	
    89	*The house's record is the soundest I have reviewed.* That was true as well, and it would be cited against the next house, to show how little a sound record weighed.
    90	
    91	He looked at the ruled margin. It was empty, and quite clean.
    92	
    93	Then he laid the pen down.
    94	
    95	He did not sign it. He did not write *seen*, or *no observation*, both of which were forms, and each of which would have meant that the office had looked and was content. He turned the leaf face down in the tray marked *done*, exactly as it had come to him, with the margin white from its head to its foot.
    96	
