   173	So there was that line, too, in a ford-keeper's book. Cael thought about it as the gelding picked his way across the stones. He thought about a girl who had come every week to a counter with an address inked on her hand, and about the one true thing a registrar had been able to give her at last: a toll paid, eastbound, in a man's name. It was the last line anybody had ever written about her father, and she had been glad to have it.
   174	
   175	There would be a line like it about them at every water from here to the last stamp. *Four, a cart, eastbound.* He did not mind. It was true, and whoever read it would learn only that they had gone.
   176	
   177	Lira looked back from the far bank, over the gelding's withers, and kept looking.
   178	
