editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:33:Cael looked at Karis. She had the notebook out at last; it had come out of her coat the moment the bar dropped, as though it had been on a spring. She wrote one word at the head of a clean leaf, and looked at it, and drew a single line beneath it. Then she looked up at the woman with the lantern, and he saw the second question arrive in her face, whole, with its grammar. It was the question of what a thing like that could be waiting for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:111:"It stops short," she said. She was sitting on the top box with the leaf on her knee. "Short of what? I can think of four answers, and I've no way of choosing among them. It may stop short of the fence, or of the people behind the fence. It may be that something lies under the ground at that distance and nowhere else. Or a hundred and ten is simply as near as it ever comes to anything, and the stone is an accident."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:133:He knew what he was looking at, and it was not anything that he knew about animals, of which he knew very little. It was his own trade. A man set to follow another is given his distance. He is told to keep it, and neither close nor fall back; and it is chosen so that the ordinary noise of the person in front will cover him, and so that one particular thing, if it should happen, will reach him across the gap. He does not act until it does. He may walk behind a subject for a year and never be given leave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:45:Brom had found his own lesson without help. A pale trunk lay stranded at the water's edge, as thick as a man's body, and he got it up on his shoulder and began to carry it along the wet stones where the footing was worst. The rule was a simple one: when the load shifted, he was to let it fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:55:He took it up again. It rolled on his shoulder at the sixth step, and Cael saw him take a decision that plainly went against the grain of his whole life, and open his hands. The trunk hit the stones and bounced, and Brom stood over it, empty-handed, with the face of a man who has let fall somebody else's child.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:215:"It may be. I'll take it, though." Brom walked a little way. "I've been told what I was worth by a district panel and a provincial counter and a letter with a red seal on it. That's the first time anybody ever said it and thought he'd had the better of the bargain."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:219:"Four lines. Nobody lost, nobody hurt, nobody knows you." Pike held up his one hand and folded the fingers down as he went. "A boat. A fence. A wagon out of a ditch. A mile behind a mule. I'd hire you tomorrow, myself, for any of the four. But a man comes to me from a hold with something that's eating his stock, and asks who he ought to have. I can't give him a crew that's never yet had anything go wrong." He let the hand fall. "It's a good book. It's a short one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:277:"Don't pass," said Lira. She had sat on a landing wall for a week learning carters, and she said it as a rule. "You don't go by a cart on a road. You fall in behind, and you wait until somebody speaks to you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:121:He had waited for the hour, and he went without his Path, as he had gone everywhere since a crossroads fire; Shadow was a declaration like any other, and he had told four people to make none. Nobody had given him the reason for the hour, and he had not sought it; but a carter of twenty-two years kept that rule, and so did a hold that had stood behind this fence for longer, and he had not lived to his age by supposing himself wiser than the people of a place. The man on the platform watched him lift the bar. He did not call down. Seln had the feeling of being entered in a column, correctly, by somebody who would not trouble him about it afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:233:*The carter says the roads are dry and the season's open: work on every line of his map from now until the snow. I have been in somebody's season since my Kindling. The registry had one, and the circuit, and the house on the bluff, and the seat at Ostrand. I never chose the day any of them began, and each one ended with a ruling about me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:287:*No instrument out here reads me. No Arbiter station, no assessment panel, no registry seat with a seal on it. The board reads what we did. Since my Kindling I've been the case the instruments couldn't parse, and it turns out the cure was a country with no instruments. Write it plainly, because it's true: I'm free. First time.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:13:*Thornwater road, the stretch. A crew to walk ahead. Nine marks.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:235:*It hears. As a dog does.* That was worth having, though not for itself. It could hear a buckle at forty-five yards, and so it had certainly heard a wired wheel, and ten feet walking, and a carter breathing through his teeth. It had heard all of them for half a mile, and it had done nothing. Whatever it was waiting for was not a sound that they were making.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:105:*Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:243:*Twenty-fourth day. Second line: forty posts, cut and set. Four marks and flour. He said "Cheap," and Brom has kept the word. Earned, six. At dusk the sealed one took my left foot for a stride, on an empty road. Rent. Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:23:"Thornwater road, the stretch. Walked ahead of one cart, one carter, one mule. Delivered at the fence. No losses. Nine marks, paid."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:247:"West of that, the inventory executes the hour any of us shows paper. Pending jurisdiction means armed. Nobody goes west."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:277:*Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:287:*No instrument out here reads me. No Arbiter station, no assessment panel, no registry seat with a seal on it. The board reads what we did. Since my Kindling I've been the case the instruments couldn't parse, and it turns out the cure was a country with no instruments. Write it plainly, because it's true: I'm free. First time.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:291:There was a thing in the grass on the Thornwater road that had gone beside them for half a mile, waiting to be told something. A whole country kept its Paths in its pockets whenever it set foot on a road, and would not say why. He had those two facts side by side in his head, as Karis had her noun and her six numbers, and he had the strong feeling that they were the two ends of one sentence and that nobody had yet given him the middle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:137:*Subject animal kept station on the party at forty-five yards for something over half a mile, without approach. Halted one hundred and ten yards from the hold's gate and remained until the gate was barred. Withdrew at a walk by the line of its coming. It was waiting upon a signal. The signal was not given. This officer does not know what the signal is.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:169:*Thornwater road, the stretch. Walked ahead of one cart, one carter, one mule. Delivered at the fence. No losses. Nine marks, paid.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:223:*Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:237:*Note the date. Season's open, and for once it isn't theirs.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:309:He wrote the day's entry short. *Fourteenth day. First line: six crossings stood, nothing lost. Earned, two. In hand, less than one, and a side of bacon. The reserve is not counted here and never will be. Thirteenth of thirteen, Lira says. Deployment since the Line: none. Pressure, Storm and Wind all offered when a tree came down the river, and Brom's Path offered too, and all four were told no.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:281:*Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:3:*Second day past the Line. One fire. Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:201:*Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:269:*Fifth day. Deployment since the Line: none. At dusk, on an empty road, Shadow got as far as my boots, seal and all. Four strides, both feet, before I had it shut. I didn't ask for it and I didn't use it for anything, so it isn't a deployment. It's what the thing costs to carry. Call it rent. Entered as rent, and Seln heard it, which is right. He would.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:319:"You're paper, aren't you," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:325:"Then you know how to keep quiet. Keep it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:365:He wrote the day's lines in what light there was. *Sixth day. Deployment since the Line: none. Ruling: hold.* Then he put the book away and ate cold flour-cake with the rest of them, in the dark, with the river going by.

