editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:57:Brom carried the boxes up the ladder by feel, one at a time, and Karis counted them from below in a voice that was not quite steady. The loft smelled of last year's barley. They lay down in it without lighting anything, and it was a long while before anybody slept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:61:"Tell me in order," said Karis. "I'll write them in the morning. I think that if I tried to hold a pencil now I should break it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:67:The fence closed off the whole head of the valley and ran on round, up one shoulder and down the other, a quarter of a mile about. Within it stood a long house, and the barn with the loft over it, and a yard for carts, and a well with a sweep; there were a dozen people or so, of every age, and a line of geese that hissed at all of them alike. The grain was the point of the place. It came up from the river country in sacks, on one man's cart, and it sat under that roof until other carts came down from holds farther east to fetch it, and the woman with the grey braid stood between the two and saw that nobody was cheated in either direction.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:69:She ran it as Bracken had run a counter. Cael saw that within an hour, and it warmed him to her more than any welcome could have done. She had a tally of the sacks on a board by the barn door, in strokes, and when Brom took the first one up the ladder she drew her charcoal through a stroke without being asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:71:There were forty-one sacks, and each weighed about as much as Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:73:Brom took them up one at a time, on his right shoulder, with his left hand for the ladder. It was the tree on the gravel bar over again, with a twelve-foot drop under it and a floor of flagstones, and Cael, who was drawing water at the well, watched every trip from the side of his eye. On the seventeenth, a rung went. It cracked through under the leading boot with a sound like the wheel had made, and the whole of Brom's weight and a sack of barley came down eighteen inches onto the rung beneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:75:He did not drop this load, for Lira was standing under him with the next one. Cael had his own breath stopped, and Anchor had already found the ladder's rail and asked whether it should be held.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:79:Brom's left arm took it. The knuckles went white and the shoulder stood out through his shirt, and the ladder bowed like a drawn bow and came back; and when he had found the rung with his foot he went on up, more slowly, and set the sack down in the loft before he looked at his own hands. They were brown, both of them. Iron Skin had not come.
