editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:119:Seln went out by the gate at noon on the forty-fifth day, when the sun stood straight over the valley and there was no shadow under the posts.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:121:He had waited for the hour, and he went without his Path, as he had gone everywhere since a crossroads fire; Shadow was a declaration like any other, and he had told four people to make none. Nobody had given him the reason for the hour, and he had not sought it; but a carter of twenty-two years kept that rule, and so did a hold that had stood behind this fence for longer, and he had not lived to his age by supposing himself wiser than the people of a place. The man on the platform watched him lift the bar. He did not call down. Seln had the feeling of being entered in a column, correctly, by somebody who would not trouble him about it afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:127:There were four prints, set square, where a thing had stood. Each was as broad as his palm and deeper at the toe than at the heel, and that depth told him how long. A creature that stands a moment leaves a mark like a seal in wax. One that stands until its weight has settled forward leaves a mark like these, and he judged that it had been there from the time a gate opened until the time an arrow went back into its case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:135:Seln had stood at that distance outside a great many doors. He had stood there for two years below a bluff, in effect, with a clerk's pen in his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:147:He considered, walking back up the ruts to the gate, whether any part of it bore upon the day in hand. That was the rule he had made for himself, and he applied it with care, since he distrusted rules that he found convenient. They were inside a fence. They would go down this valley again behind a cart, at noon, with the sun over them. On that day there was nothing in his eight roads that five people needed in order to put one foot before the other, and so he would keep it, as he had kept the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:151:In the meantime he walked about with it, and listened for the level voice. *What did you see?* The woman of the hold did not ask it. She had watched him go out at noon and she would watch him come in, and she plainly thought that a man's errands at that stone were his own. The archer did not ask. The boy looked at the dust on his boots each evening, and at the direction it had come from, and wrote down, Seln was fairly certain, only that there had been dust.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:155:Seln found that he liked him very much, and went in to carry sacks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:159:The carter was back on the fourth day, an hour past noon, with the mule in a lather.
