editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:165:"From the one-armed man. He's wrote it in the book, and that's it copied. He says you're to read it, and if it's wrong you can tell him so when you're down, and he'll say it over for the meadow after." The carter rubbed his neck. "He never sends silver up a road. It's in his box."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:169:*Thornwater road, the stretch. Walked ahead of one cart, one carter, one mule. Delivered at the fence. No losses. Nine marks, paid.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:171:"He's counted the mule," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:177:Lira read it twice with her lips moving, and then looked up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:181:Cael went through it once more, a word at a time, as he would have gone through an instrument. There had been one cart. They had walked ahead of it. Everything that had gone in at the mouth of that valley had come to a fence. He could not find a word in it that he would have changed, and what struck him harder was that he could not find one that said anything about him at all, beyond what he had done on a particular evening.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:185:"*No losses,*" said Karis. She had it already, on her own leaf. "On the ferry he wrote *nothing lost*. That was casks. Here the noun has changed, and it's because of what there was to lose. He is very exact. I wonder whether he knows how exact he is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:187:The carter had been looking at his off wheel while they read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:189:It had held, all the way down the valley and all the way back, and it was an ugly piece of work: a thorn peg for a pin, and a rim wound round with wire like a mended pot. Brom had been looking at it as well. He had looked at it every time he crossed the yard for three days, with the expression of a man who has left a knot tied wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:193:There were both. They did it that afternoon in the cart-yard, the two of them, with the wheel off and laid across a pair of trestles. Brom cut the split timber out of the rim and shaped a new one to the curve with a drawknife, taking a shaving at a time and trying it and taking another, and the carter held what he was told to hold. It took them until the shadows were long. When the wheel was on again Brom spun it, and stood with his head bent, listening; it ran round four full turns and never ticked once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:195:The carter looked at the wheel for a while. Then he went to the front of his cart, and reached in under the seat board, and came back with a roll of something pale tied round with a thong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:199:It was a calfskin, scraped thin and gone the colour of cream. Karis had it out of Brom's hands before he had finished untying it. She unrolled it on the tailboard, and held the corners down with her spread fingers, and for a moment did not breathe.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:205:"That's twenty-two years," said the carter. He sounded almost embarrassed. "I've the same in my head, so I'll not miss it. You've been going about with a clever girl and three boxes of paper and not a road among you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:207:"I shall keep it dry," said Karis. Her voice had gone very small. "I shall keep it flat, and dry, and I shall not let anybody put a cup on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:217:He had not written much at Thornwater. The first night he had not trusted his hand, and since then he had drawn water, and carried what Brom could not, and helped Karis to pace out a number along a fence. He was eighteen, and for three days he had been a hired man on a grain hold, and it had suited him better than he would have guessed. But there was a scrap of bark in the pocket of Hesk's book now, where two letters had lain, and it seemed to him that the book ought to know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:219:*Forty-sixth day past the Line. Thornwater: a grain hold at the head of a valley, two days east of the board. Fourth line, in Pike's hand, copied and brought up by the carter. Nine marks, in his box. Earned, eighteen. In hand tonight, a few coppers and a map.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:221:*On the forty-third, at dusk, a thing the hold calls a stillhound went beside us for the last half of a mile. Five counts and a distance, given to Karis; she has the name and I have the numbers and neither of us has a reason. Nobody declared anything. Nobody drew. I put all nine down at every stride and they kept getting up.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:227:The carter had said it as he might have said that the hay was in. *That's the season open.* He had meant the roads, and the weather on them, and a board in a meadow with slips enough on it for every crew that could walk. He could not have known what the word had been to the person he said it to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:233:*The carter says the roads are dry and the season's open: work on every line of his map from now until the snow. I have been in somebody's season since my Kindling. The registry had one, and the circuit, and the house on the bluff, and the seat at Ostrand. I never chose the day any of them began, and each one ended with a ruling about me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:241:Down in the yard Brom was spinning a wheel again, for the pleasure of hearing nothing, with a row of geese drawn up behind him like a jury. Lira sat on the well's coping, binding the hilt of her knife. On the platform by the gate the bowman stood as he always stood, with his bow at the length of his arm, looking down the valley at a white stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:243:Cael looked where he was looking. There was nothing beside the stone, and the light was going. He buckled the book into the satchel and climbed down the ladder, to draw the evening's water before it was dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:3:They stayed six days at Thornwater, and went down on the forty-eighth morning in front of an empty cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:5:The carter was going back for his next load, and the boxes rode with him again, on a bed of folded sacks, since nobody had been asked to pay for them the first time. He took the stretch at noon. Cael walked the mile of it with the sun on the crown of his head, in the left-hand rut, with his knife on his belt and all nine of his hands held down; and he saw grass, and a split cart-shaft, and a white stone with nothing beside it. The frogs creaked from one end of the valley to the other and did not stop anywhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:9:There were twice the tents, for the dry roads had done it, as the carter had said they would: crews had come in from every quarter while the five were away, and the grass along the river was trodden brown, and three fires burned where there had been one. A knot of people stood at the plank's near end, under the long eave, as people stand at a counter when a clerk is giving out results.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:23:"Thornwater road, the stretch. Walked ahead of one cart, one carter, one mule. Delivered at the fence. No losses. Nine marks, paid."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:25:It was strange to hear. Cael had read those words on a scrap of bark in a hold's yard, and thought them his; and now they belonged to a woman with a board of loaves on her head, and a man mending a trace, and an old carter under a tree, who had lifted his chin at the word *stretch* and held it there to the end. Several of the people at the plank turned and looked at the five of them. They looked once, at their boots and their steel, and then went back to their own affairs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:27:Pike unlocked the iron box and counted nine coins onto the oak, in threes. Karis took them up, since the purse was hers to keep. She did not count them a second time in front of him, which Cael knew she would do later.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:31:"North road. Four carts of salt and iron walked to the upper holds. Delivered at the last gate. One mule lamed. Fourteen marks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:39:"Near fore. He was pointing it in the yard before a pack went on. His own carter will tell you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:45:He wrote it so, and when the ink was on the page he straightened, and turned his shoulder, and said the whole line through once more, from *north road* to *paid*, with the four words changed. Only then did he go to the box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:47:"Why twice?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:55:Pike looked past her, down the length of the meadow. There were perhaps two hundred people in it that afternoon, cooking and mending and arguing over mules, and Cael watched him consider them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:95:"He'd only have written what was said," Lira whispered. "That's all he was going to do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:103:The plank was nearly full. The dry weather had brought slips in from everywhere, and there was hardly a hand's breadth of bare oak between the posts; he read along it by habit, the bark and the hide and the pictures drawn for people who had no letters. At the right-hand post his eye stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:105:It was a piece of real paper, the first he had seen there since the waybill. There was a mark at its foot that he had watched a woman make with a charred stick, at a gate, by lantern light: a thorn twig, three spines to a side, and a wavy line under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:113:"Forty," said Lira, beside him. Her voice cracked on it. "Forty *marks*. That's more than twice everything we've got."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:115:"Clearance of what?" said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:117:"I know of what," said Karis. "I have the word for it underlined."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:121:"What's HOLD?" said Lira, across the plank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:139:Lira was frowning at the name, and Cael knew the frown, since he had seen it turned on every card she had ever stood under.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:149:"But why would anybody not *want* it written?" Lira's hand had gone to the breast of her coat, where a certificate was. "I'm Iron, Rank One. I bled for that. I'd have it on my forehead."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:153:"Copper," said Brom. He had not been asked, and he said it as a man lays down a thing he has been carrying since a gate. "For good. There's a finding that says otherwise, on a table, and I'll never sign it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:157:"Same as me, then," he said. He set the stone a little higher on the page. "I'll tell you what I tell every one that comes over that hill with a number in his coat. A Bronze who lost my carter is worth less than a Copper who brought him home. Out here that's arithmetic. In there it was heresy."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:171:Brom's rule allowed the jug, and it was Brom who said so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:173:He had turned it over all the way down from the saddle, Cael thought. The rule was that they ate what the board paid, and the board had that day paid nine marks into Karis's hand, and there was flour in the bag and bacon. He stood in the trodden grass outside the long eave and worked it through with his lips, and then he held up one finger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:183:It was one sheet, as Lira had told them: a piece of sized sailcloth as tall as a child, pinned to the plaster at its four corners, and written from top to bottom in the square black hand. He had said that he would read it when there was something on it that he had done. He went and stood in front of it now, with his thumbs in his belt, and began at the top, as he began everything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:191:He stood there for some time, since a board in Ardenmere had carried him, once, with a question mark where the letters and the number should have been, and he had felt that question mark between his shoulders in every street of the district. This said only that there were five of them, and where they came in a list of thirteen. He looked for what it thought of him and could not find that it thought anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:195:He had a place, on the hearth bench, under the sheet, and nobody was sitting in it. Somebody put a cup in his hand before he was fairly down. Cael watched him drink off half of it, and then, because the thought had been with him since a scrap of bark in a hold's yard, he took his own cup and went over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:213:Lira had come up on his other side. She had been standing in front of the sheet, as he had, a good deal longer and a good deal less quietly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:225:Brom had not come over, and sat at their own boards with the jug in front of him, looking up the wall with his lips moving. He started at the bottom and went to the top. Then he did it again, from the top down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:231:Karis moved the jug before she would unroll the hide.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:235:"There was a slip," said Lira. "On the plank, by the left post, on a shingle. Did you see it? A chest to be carried to the turf fire. *Eleven marks.*" She put her finger on the ring with the bar through it and ran it off the edge of the hide, toward the door. "That's a road we know. We know both fords on it. We know where the bad water is, and which keeper counts hay twice. It's nine days and nobody on it but deer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:237:"It's eleven marks," said Brom slowly. "That's more than the stretch paid."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:241:Seln had been sitting a little back from the lamp, as he sat a little back from anything. He leaned forward now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:245:He had told them, on a shelf of turf, that he knew a certain phrase, having copied it onto other people's files; and none of them had asked him then what it kept in its drawer. He put one finger down on the hide's western edge, on bare skin, an inch beyond the place where the carter's ruts ran out, and he kept it there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:249:"Nobody wants to," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:251:"Nobody can," said Karis. "Different sentences."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:253:She said it in her small dry voice, as she would have corrected a citation. Lira took her finger off the map.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:255:That was all. Nobody at that table spoke of it again, that night or for a long time after. Seln lifted his hand from the edge of the hide. Then he took the skin by two of its corners and turned it on the boards, a half circle, so that the ring with the bar through it lay nearest the door, and everything else that a carter had drawn in twenty-two years ran away from them toward the hearth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:257:It lay with the east uppermost, and Karis put her fingers back on the corners.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:261:"I have walked the first part of every one," said Seln, mildly, and picked up his cup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:263:Cael looked at him. Seln looked into the cup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:271:*Forty-ninth day. Lowmarch. The Thornwater line was said for the meadow and paid: nine. Earned, eighteen. In hand, about ten, after one jug, which the board bought and Brom allowed. The reserve is not counted here.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:275:*Asked Pike whether he had heard of Vell. No. Seln put a finger on the west edge of the map and said what's there. Once. The map is turned east now.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:281:Brom was pouring the last of the jug into five cups, with great fairness, a little at a time. Lira had gone back to the sheet and was standing in front of it with her arms folded and her chin up. Karis was copying a ring with a bar through it onto a leaf of her own. Seln had his eyes shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:283:Nobody in the room was watching him. He checked it, as he checked it everywhere, and the count came out at nothing, and for the first time it did not go round again by itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:287:*No instrument out here reads me. No Arbiter station, no assessment panel, no registry seat with a seal on it. The board reads what we did. Since my Kindling I've been the case the instruments couldn't parse, and it turns out the cure was a country with no instruments. Write it plainly, because it's true: I'm free. First time.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:291:There was a thing in the grass on the Thornwater road that had gone beside them for half a mile, waiting to be told something. A whole country kept its Paths in its pockets whenever it set foot on a road, and would not say why. He had those two facts side by side in his head, as Karis had her noun and her six numbers, and he had the strong feeling that they were the two ends of one sentence and that nobody had yet given him the middle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:313:Two carts gone, a month back. He knew the stretch, and what that mile asked of a person's hands, and he knew that the ford on that road was a worse place than the grass. It wanted clearing, and it wanted clearing by somebody who would not lose a man doing it; and there were, by his reckoning, two crews on the sheet who could, and the other was up the north road until the new moon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:321:He had heard their four said, or heard of them. A boat, a fence, the hide-woman's wagon. And today the stretch, walked at dusk, by the carter's own telling, with a hound pacing them for half the mile; and not one of the five had so much as lifted a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:327:The girl was standing at the sheet, and he read her from the feet. She had her weight on the balls of them, even standing still, even with her arms folded, and her heels hardly marked the rushes. Somebody had spent years teaching those feet to leave a floor in a hurry. They had been taught on a chalked floor, too, with someone watching to give a mark, for she stood as if she were being scored. She was fast, and proud of it, and she had not learned yet that out here nobody was going to tell her so; he thought that it would cost her something before she did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:331:The small pale one had a notebook open on the table, in an inn, among cups, and a skin of some sort pinned under her fingers. As he watched she copied something from the one to the other, and looked at what she had written, and ruled a line beneath it with real satisfaction.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:361:He was a man of forty or so, of the middle height, heavy in the forearm, with hair cropped close and gone grey over the ears. He had stopped on the sill before he came in. That was the first item, and Cael put the most weight on it. Three people had stopped behind him in the same instant, at the same distances, without a word; and for the length of a breath the man's eyes had gone nowhere at all, while he listened to them be there. He had counted his own before he counted the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:367:He had read Lira, and it had taken him three breaths. Brom had hardly taken him one. He had looked at Karis ruling her line, and something had happened at the corner of his mouth and been put down again, quickly, like a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:369:Then he had stopped, and Cael had watched him do it. The man's eyes had finished with the table and come to rest, and a small crease had come between them, and they had gone back to the left and begun again. Cael had seen officers, and watchers, and a hooded seat, and a Gold on a stone floor look at the five of them, and every one had counted four. This one had found, on a second pass, a mild man in a dark coat with his eyes shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:3:By the forty-first day they had three lines in Pike's book and a place at the edge of the carters' fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:5:Nobody had offered it to them. A fire burned most nights in the middle of the meadow, a little way from the ash, and the people with carts sat round it on their own shafts and upturned buckets. For a month the five had kept to their own fire, forty yards off, as they had kept forty yards behind three carts on a road. Then, on the evening after the wash, the woman with the hides had moved her bucket a foot to the left without looking at anybody, and it had left a gap in the ring. Brom had looked at the gap for a whole day before he would let them sit in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:7:Nine marks earned, the book said. It did not say that Lira's palms had skinned over, or that Brom could now drop a tree at the first slip and catch it again at the second, or that Karis had held a point unlit to the count of three hundred and then put it out herself. Cael kept those in his own book, at the foot of each day, under a line that had not changed since a ring of churned turf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:9:The carter came in at dusk with one cart and one mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:11:He was a short, thick man, burnt as brown as a nut, and his cart was loaded to the rails with sacks. He chocked his wheels, saw to the mule, and sat down in the ring with a yard of empty ground on either side of him. Cael had seen him once already that day, at noon, handing a slip across the plank; and he had watched the meadow read that slip all afternoon, crew after crew, and go back to its dinner.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:15:"Nine," Lira had said, at the plank. "For walking. That's as much as we've made since we got here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:17:"And nobody's taken it," Brom had said. "So there's a thing we don't know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:19:The carter ate what he had brought. He did it slowly, looking into the fire, and when he had done he wiped his knife on his knee and spoke to the flames.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:21:"Two carts went quiet on it last month," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:31:"What's on it?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:33:She had asked it clearly, across the fire, as she would have asked a coach what a drill was for. Cael felt Karis go still beside him, with her pencil half out of its spine. Several of the people in the ring looked at the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:35:The carter turned his head and considered Lira for some time. He did not seem offended. He seemed to be working out how much of an answer a person was owed who knew no better than to ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:41:"It's what there is." He looked past her, at the rest of them, and his eyes stopped longest on Brom. "You're the ones had her wagon out of the wash. Before the water. And never lit, she says, nor shouted, nor one of you did a thing but pull."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:49:Cael did what he always did. The claim was a mile of road that carters crossed only at one hour of the day. Under it stood two carts that had not come home, and a price that the whole meadow thought too small, and a word, *quiet*, that everybody out here used for a thing done and also for a thing that happened to a cart. He had no notion what walked on that stretch. But a man who had kept the road for twenty-two years had just said what he wanted for it, and it was the one thing they had spent a month learning to do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:53:Brom's went up first, and then Seln's. Lira's followed, with her jaw set, for she had not forgiven the answer. Karis kept hers down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:55:"The boxes," she said. "I left them three days in that loft, for a fence, and I didn't sleep. This is two days out and I can't tell how many back."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:57:The carter had heard her. Out here, it seemed, everybody heard everything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:59:"What boxes?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:67:Karis put her hand up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:73:He had made them a sign, since five names would not go on a scrap of bark: five short strokes side by side, like a tally with its bar left off. He drew it under the carter's own mark and read the whole aloud to an empty meadow, and then he handed it across the plank and looked at each of them twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:81:They went out by the east road, over the saddle. Beyond the top the country changed, as Seln had said it would: the road shrank to two ruts in a grey scrub that stood as high as a man's belt, and the mule cart, which had looked a toy in the meadow, fitted those ruts as a foot fits a boot. The three boxes rode on the sacks under their own canvas. Karis walked beside the off wheel, where she could have put her hand on them, and never did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:85:Lira looked at the stump as she went by, and smiled with one side of her mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:87:Nobody spoke for the whole of that day, and Cael found that it was no longer anything he did on purpose. Brom carried the bill across his shoulder with the blade out. Lira's knife rode on her hip. At noon they sat in the ruts and ate cold cake, and the carter, who had watched them out of the side of his eye since the saddle, stopped watching.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:89:The mule took to Brom at the first halt. It walked the length of its tether to reach him and laid the whole weight of its long head on his shoulder, and sighed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:91:"He doesn't do that," said the carter. It was the first thing he had said since the plank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:93:"They do, with me." Brom stood quite still under it. "I've never known why. I think it's that I'm the same height as a post."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:95:Cael watched the carter at that halt, and at the next, and learned more from his hands than he had from his mouth. The man carried a lump of mutton fat in a rag, and he worked it into both axles every time the cart stood, though neither had begun to complain. His trace chains were bound in strips of old sacking from end to end, so that they could not ring. He had cut the iron heel-plates off his own boots, a long time ago by the look of the leather, and when the mule threw up its head at a bird he laid two fingers on its nose before it could think of making a remark. He had been asked what was on his road, and had declined to say. But everything he owned had been taught not to make a sound, and Cael supposed that was an answer too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:97:They camped cold, in a hollow, a good way back from a stream. Lira and Seln took the first watch and Brom and Karis the second, and it was Cael's night to be the fifth, which he spent lying on his back, hearing every sound the scrub made and failing to sleep through any of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:101:They had forded the valley's water twice by then, at shallow places with no name, in good light, and the carter had begun to walk with his shoulders down. Then the off wheel gave a crack like a stick breaking. It ran on three turns more, wobbling, and came off its axle altogether, and the cart sat down on that corner with a jolt that sent Karis's hand to the canvas at last.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:105:Brom looked at it. Then he looked at the carter, who was looking at the sun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:107:"How long?" said the carter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:111:He had wire. They got a pole under the axle, and Brom lifted, with the cords standing in his neck and no grey in him anywhere, while Seln and Cael built stones up under the bed. He whittled a new pin out of thorn with the hedging bill, and bound the split rim round and round, and twisted the wire tight with the back of the blade. Karis timed him, since nobody had told her not to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:115:The carter said nothing at all. He took the mule's head, and they went on faster than before, and the wired wheel ticked at every turn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:123:The carter had stopped the mule. He stood with his fist in its cheek-strap and looked up the valley, and he spoke for the second time that day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:127:"We could camp," said Brom. "Short of it. And go through at noon."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:133:"A man did," said the carter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:137:Seln had been looking at the grass, in sections, from the near ground outward. He turned round, and for once he did not wait for anybody to look at him first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:139:"I shall say where I want each of you, and I should be obliged if nobody argued until we are at the far end." His voice was hardly louder than the wind. "I go ahead, thirty paces, on the left verge. The boy and Lira walk ten paces in front of the mule, one to a rut, and you do not look at each other; you look outward. The carter keeps his beast's head. Karis is at its off shoulder, where the cart is between her and whatever is nearest. Brom is behind the tailboard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:141:"Behind," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:143:"A thing that follows a cart comes up on its tail. I should like it to find you there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:145:"And what do we do, if it does?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:147:"What he has paid for. We walk." Seln looked at the carter's fist on the strap. "Everything you carry that has an edge, carry it where it shows. Nothing else. I mean that as exactly as I have ever meant anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:153:The cart had its own noises, and he learned them, since they were the only ones he could do nothing about. The wired wheel ticked. The grain shifted in its sacks. And on the mule's harness, somewhere behind the saddle-pad, a loose buckle clicked against its own tongue at every step.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:155:He walked in the left rut with his knife on his belt and looked outward, as he had been told. The sun touched the ridge behind him. His shadow went out in front, forty feet long, and Lira's beside it, and then quite suddenly there were no shadows.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:157:A little way past that, on the left, something stood up out of the grass that was not grass. It was a cart-shaft. There was only the one, grey and split, pointing at the sky at the angle of a man's raised arm, and whatever it had once been fastened to lay down in the stems where nobody could see it. The carter did not turn his head as they went by. Karis did, and then looked very steadily at the mule's ears.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:159:Lira put her hand up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:161:She did not stop walking. She only raised her right hand to the height of her shoulder, with the fingers shut, and held it there. She had spent her life listening to moving air and knew its sounds better than a carter knows his mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:169:"Don't declare anything," said Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:175:He meant a Path. He meant Lira's burst, and Brom's Iron Skin, and any point that Karis might set; and Cael knew, with a coldness in his stomach, that he meant nine fragments as well, which were nobody's Path at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:187:"I could clear it," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:191:"No," said Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:201:Karis was at the mule's off shoulder with her face as white as her fingers. Her right hand had gone to the breast of her coat, where the notebook was. It stopped there. It came down. He saw it go up again within ten strides, and stop on the same button, and come down; she was a person watching the one thing in a month that she most wanted to write, at the one moment when taking out a pencil might be a thing this country heard. He counted, since counting was what he had. She decided against it five times in that mile.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:203:Behind her the carter walked with his palm spread over the mule's nostrils, and the mule's ears lay back along its neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:205:And behind the tailboard came Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:207:Cael had seen him fill a doorway and had seen him fill a district's frame. He had seen him make a harness room feel small by standing up in it. He had never seen this. Brom was walking with the bill held low across his thighs, and his arms were brown to the wrist, and somehow he had drawn in everything about himself that was more than bone and muscle and a coat. He was exactly as large as his body and no larger. It was still a good deal of body, and it was all there was, and it walked at the pace of a mule and looked at nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:211:Wind had his feet first. They had leaned for a month, a dozen times a day, and they leaned now toward the rise as if the ground sloped that way. Pressure began to gather under his breastbone, slowly, a breath at a time. The Iron read wanted to open and tell him the mass of everything within a pace and a half, which was nothing but a mule; it did not feel like doing anything, and he shut it. Ember offered a point in the dry grass at the foot of the swell, and behind it another and another, so that he could see how the whole slope would take fire along a line. Anchor had found the wired rim and asked about it. Blade wanted Brom's bill. Storm turned over, once, like a great fish under a boat, and showed him the air between himself and the grey thing as a lane already laid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:213:And it was dusk. Shadow came up behind all the rest, soft, the oldest of his refusals, with the only offer that he had ever truly wanted to take. It could make less of him. He could go up that valley and not be there at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:225:So, first: it kept its station. He checked that three times. The ruts bent a little to the right, toward the swell, and the grey shape gave ground, drifting up the slope until there were forty-five yards between them again. The ruts bent left, and it came down. Whatever it wanted, it wanted it from exactly that far away, like a watcher who has been told his distance.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:227:Second: it made no sound. He had known that from the grass. Now he saw the other half of it, which was that the frogs went dumb a little way in front of it and took up again a little way behind, so that it walked the whole time inside the hole that Lira had heard. He did not know how frogs had learned to do that. He thought it would take a long time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:229:The third thing he nearly missed. Its head was not turned toward the people. All the way up the valley it had been turned a little behind him, toward the cart, and when he looked for the reason he heard it: the buckle. It was the one thing in the whole party that kept time. He lifted his left hand and closed the fist, twice, where Brom would see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:233:The grey head swung. It went slowly to one side and then to the other, low, casting, as a dog casts that has lost a line. Brom let go. The buckle clicked, and the head came round and settled on it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:235:*It hears. As a dog does.* That was worth having, though not for itself. It could hear a buckle at forty-five yards, and so it had certainly heard a wired wheel, and ten feet walking, and a carter breathing through his teeth. It had heard all of them for half a mile, and it had done nothing. Whatever it was waiting for was not a sound that they were making.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:239:A gully came down out of the right-hand hill, and at its foot the floods of other years had spread a fan of pale stones clean across the valley, a hundred feet wide, with a skin of water shining on it. The ruts ran straight over. The mule's feet rattled in the wet, and the cart came after with a noise like a sack of nails. Out on the right the grey shape came to the edge of the fan and stopped as if it had met a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:243:He did not know why. He set that down, in his head, with a ruled line under it, for Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:247:It had done nothing. It had answered nothing. For a mile it had gone beside six people and a mule like a man kept waiting outside a door. Such a man has been told that a word will be said, and that he is to do nothing until he hears it. They had not said the word. He did not know what the word was. But he had put down nine hands at every stride for a mile, and Lira had held her feet, and Karis her pencil, and Brom had been only a large man with a hedging tool. Not one Path had been declared on that road, and not one fragment had answered; and so the thing had been given nothing, and had reacted to nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:251:The fence stood up out of the grass all at once, as the carter had said it would: split posts, as high as a man's head, from one shoulder of the valley to the other, with a gate in the middle and a lantern coming toward the gate from inside. He heard a bar drawn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:255:It had not sat, or turned. It stood in the thin grass with its head level, beside a white stone, and it came no farther. Cael fixed the stone. Then he counted his own strides from abreast of it to the gate, as Karis would have counted, with his lips shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:259:Nobody had struck a blow, or so much as drawn a knife. Behind him he heard Brom let out a breath that he seemed to have been keeping since the mouth of the valley. The gate swung inward on a leather hinge, and the lantern came up, and behind it he saw a woman's hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:3:Brom laid it out for them in the morning, on the tailboard, with oats.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:5:He had been awake a good while, as Cael knew by the gelding, who had been watered and picked out and now stood with a full net in front of him, and by the tailboard itself, which had been swept clean. On the clean oak Brom had set down four small heaps of grain in a row and a fifth by itself, and he waited until all of them were looking before he began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:7:"That's a day," he said, and touched the four. "Four nets, on this grass, for a horse his size, and the boy wasn't lying to me. I've watched him eat three since yesterday noon and look round for the next." He moved one finger to the fifth heap. "That's the copper to stand the cart. And none of it's us. We haven't had a mouthful out of that yet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:9:"What does it come to?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:11:"There's a slip on that plank with a seal on it. Two marks, for a crew to stand on a boat all day." He put his palm flat over the four heaps and the fifth together. "He eats that in two days. So does the cart, standing. Every second morning one of those fourteen slips goes down a horse, and we've done the work for it, and we're no further on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:13:Nobody spoke at once, and the gelding pulled at his net, loudly, as though to make the point for him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:15:"He draws the cart," said Lira. "That's what he's for. You can't carry three boxes on your back to wherever the work is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:19:It was Seln who had asked, mildly, from his place a little behind the rest; and when they turned he was looking at the eastern saddle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:21:"I walked a short way last evening, and again before it was light," he said. "I do not make a practice of saying where, but this bears upon the horse. The east road is a cart's width as far as the top of that rise; beyond it there are two ruts cut in scrub, and I could span the pair of them with my arms. The north road is the same once it has passed the last roof." He considered the cart with something like regret. "It was built for a metalled way with a ditch on either side, and it will go back along the road it came by, or it will stand in this meadow; I do not think there is a third thing it can do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:23:"The carts here are half the width," said Karis slowly. "I wrote that down on the sixth day and never asked myself what it meant."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:25:"And there is the other matter." Seln turned his cup in his hands. "The courier looked at his feet before she looked at us, and so did the woman with the hides, and the boy at the ferry, and the boy with the tally. A shod horse on these roads is a notice, in large type, and what it says is the word the old man used. I should prefer not to walk into every hold east of here behind it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:27:Lira had her arms folded, and she was looking at the gelding and not at any of them, and Cael, who had watched her lose bouts, knew that she had already seen how this one would be scored.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:29:"He did a hill a day," she said. "Every day. She said he would, and he did. He went through three rivers and down a scree on his own feet with a cart sliding behind him, and he never once lay down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:31:"He didn't," said Brom. "I'm not saying he's a bad horse. I'm saying he's a horse."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:35:"You told him you'd sell him to one," said Brom. "In the brown river, the second day out. The first one past the Line, you said. I was standing at the tailboard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:39:"What is his name?" said Karis. She had her notebook open. "I ask because I have been writing *the gelding* for a fortnight, and if there is to be a sale I should like the entry to be right."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:43:"He hasn't got one," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:47:"You don't name what you sell," said Brom gently.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:49:"I didn't name him." Lira's chin came up. "I only called him things."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:51:"I have a number of them written down," said Karis. "I don't think any will do for a bill of sale."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:53:Brom made a sound that he turned into a cough, and Lira looked round at the four of them with her mouth gone very tight at one corner; and then, since nothing else could be done with it, she laughed. It was not a long laugh, and when it was over she wiped her nose on her wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:69:"A horse," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:75:The ferryman came out from behind the mule, wiping his hands on his thighs, and walked round the gelding once. He ran a hand down each leg in turn and lifted the feet, and then he took hold of the upper lip, not roughly, and looked in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:79:"He's experienced," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:87:Lira drew breath, but Brom spoke first, as Cael had known he would, since it was not in him to let a man buy a load without being told what it weighed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:95:They put him to the beam, and he stood there with the collar on and his ears back, plainly waiting for somebody to show him the slope. Then the ferryman clicked his tongue, and the gelding leaned, and the drum groaned and began to turn. He went a quarter of the way round and stopped, and looked at the ground in front of him, which was flat; then he went on again, and at the half he stopped once more, and found it flat there as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:99:"Look at him," said Lira bitterly. "A fortnight I've hauled that animal over a continent, and talked myself hoarse at him, and all he ever wanted was to go round and round a post."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:101:"He likes knowing where it ends," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:107:They settled the price on an upturned bucket, Brom and the ferryman, and it took a good while, because Brom had counted the old mule's stops. There had been eleven in a single crossing, the day before, and he knew how many carts had stood waiting on the far bank through each one, and he set that sum against the gelding's teeth and went on setting it there, patiently, until the ferryman stopped talking about teeth. Cael did not hear the figure, though he heard what went with it, for that had been his own notion: dry room for three boxes in the loft above the drum, where the ferryman's boy slept, for as long as the horse walked underneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:109:"And the mule?" said Brom, when they had shaken on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:119:"I didn't know," said Brom. "So I asked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:121:Lira said her goodbye in her own way. She stood at the edge of the trodden ring with her arms folded and told the gelding, as he came round, that he was a great idle brute who had been bought for more than he was worth and sold for more than that, and had now fallen into the one employment in all the world without a hill in it, having done nothing whatever to deserve it. He did not turn his head, for he was looking at the ground in front of him, with his ears forward, and it was flat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:123:The cart went to the smith the same afternoon. He was a square, silent man, and he wanted it for the iron on its wheels and the oak in its bed, and said he would cut the axle down to a mule's gauge and let it out by the day to anybody fool enough to want wheels on those roads. Brom emptied it first, and from under the seat board he drew out a hedging bill with a hooked blade and an ash handle worn dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:125:"That goes with the cart," said the smith.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:127:"It came with the cart. It isn't the cart." Brom turned the blade over and looked along its edge. "I've a use for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:129:The smith shrugged, and then sold Lira a long knife, which was the last thing the old purse ever bought. She chose it by its balance, across two fingers, as she would have chosen a lath; and when he told her that it was a skinning blade she said that it was whatever she said it was, and wore it home on her hip, outside her coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:133:They sealed the money that night, by the fire, with the meadow going quiet round them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:135:Karis counted it. She had the purse from Ostrand in her lap, and the ferryman's price and the smith's on a cloth beside her, and she counted the whole twice over in her small dry voice, under her breath, and wrote one figure at the foot of a leaf. Then she did a thing Cael had never seen her do with a number, which was to turn the notebook over on her knee, face down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:139:"Go on at what?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:143:"That's my rule, and I'd better say it," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:145:He sat on one of the boxes, which he had carried up a ladder into the loft that afternoon and then carried down again for this, since Karis would not have the bag sealed out of her sight, and his hands hung between his knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:147:"We eat what the board pays. Only that. What's in that cloth doesn't buy a loaf, or a net, or a night under a roof, however wet it is." He looked round at them. "It's there for a leg that's broken, or a box that's burned, or a morning when one of us has to be got somewhere fast. It isn't for dinner."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:149:"We've more silver in that bag than this whole meadow has seen," said Lira, "and you want us living on two marks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:155:"I can give you one reason," said Seln, "and it is the least of them. A crew that spends silver in a place like this has told the place where it came from. We have just sold a horse so as not to say so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:157:"The estate's paid me a son's allowance since I was fourteen," said Brom. "Every month, whether I answered the letter or I didn't. I never earned a copper of it, and I never went hungry, and I can't tell you what I'm worth to anybody, because nobody's ever had to pay it. The registry says Copper and a district panel says Iron, and neither of them has ever paid me either." He turned his big hands over and looked at the palms. "There's a man here with one arm who'll write down what I did and what it fetched. I'd like to read that line without knowing there's a bag behind it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:159:Karis nodded once, as she nodded at a citation.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:161:"Mine's shorter," said Cael. "Most of what I put in was Hesk's. He sent it a little at a time for two years, folded inside his letters, out of a shop that two polite men keep calling at. It was never mine to eat. I'd like to send some of it back one day and tell him that I didn't need it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:163:Lira looked at the fire for a while, and then she nodded, slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:167:So Karis sewed the bag. She had a sailmaker's needle in her kit, for the spines of notebooks, and she closed the canvas with a flat seam and went back over it, and Brom warmed his wax at the fire and laid it along the stitches in a strip as wide as a thumb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:171:Lira pressed hers first, hard, and Karis followed her, and Brom, whose print took up a third of the strip, and Cael; and then the wax was cooling, and one of them had not moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:173:"I have gone fifteen years without leaving one of those on anything," said Seln. He was looking at the strip with real interest. "It was the second thing I was taught. I find that the hand does not want to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:175:"It's in the table," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:181:"Mm," said Seln, and he leaned forward and put his thumb into the wax beside the other four, and held it there until the edge had set.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:183:Brom carried the box back up the ladder with the bag at the very bottom of it, under a layer of masonry chips. When he came down, Seln had taken the surveyor's case out from between the other two boxes and was putting its strap over his head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:185:"It rode under a canvas for ten days," he said, to nobody. "I had thought I might sleep the better for it, and I did not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:187:Brom went through the food bag by the last of the light and told them what was in it: oats, and a heel of the last keeper's cheese, and flour for six days if nobody was greedy. All of that had been bought before the wax went on, and they agreed that it was theirs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:189:"Six days," said Lira. "To be worth two marks to somebody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:191:"Five," said Brom. "I'm greedy."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:203:"Stand," said Lira. "That's the work."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:207:So they stood, and Seln placed them without being asked: one at each forward corner, and one aft on either side, and the fifth amidships by the goods, where the casks were. He changed the fifth at every crossing, as he changed the sleeper on a watch, and Cael noticed that he never once put himself where the sun would be behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:215:"You're smiling," said Karis. She was amidships, with her notebook, timing the crossing by Hesk's clock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:221:Lira had been looking at the shed as well, at every crossing, and had not said one word about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:227:"The cable," said Seln sharply. "If the roots take the cable it will have us round."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:233:Brom had the sweep, which was a pole of ash twenty feet long, shod with iron, and he had it out of its crutch and over the upstream rail before the boy had finished shouting. He set the iron against the trunk, just ahead of the roots, and the tree came on and leaned its whole wet weight into him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:235:Cael saw his forearms.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:237:It was Iron Skin, which was the whole of Brom's Path, and he had seen it come a hundred times, in a harness room and in a river: the skin ceased to crease, and went the grey of a hearthstone, from the wrist upward. It began now, and it got as far as the cuff of his coat before Brom looked down at his own arm, as a man looks at a dog that has got up on a chair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:243:"Like a large man," said Brom, breathing hard, and looking at his forearms, which were red. "It's heavier that way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:259:"He says everything twice," said Lira in Cael's ear. "He looks at you twice and he reads it twice. Is it his age?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:267:There was an iron box bolted to the nearer post, under the plank, with a lock on it the size of a fist. Pike opened it with a key from round his neck and took out two coins, and laid them on the oak in front of Brom, who happened to be standing nearest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:269:They were silver, small and thick, and a good deal worn, and Brom picked them up and held them in the middle of his palm, and went on looking at them after Pike had locked the box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:273:"Nothing." Brom closed his hand. "It's the first money I've ever had that nobody sent me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:277:"Well, it spends the same," he said. "That's the pity of it." But he went on watching Brom's back for a little while as they walked away down the meadow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:281:It bought flour, and salt, and a piece of bacon no bigger than a hand, and Brom told them that it would have kept the gelding for one day and the half of another. Karis wrote the line into the field archive as she had heard it said, and drew her second rule under two of its words.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:285:Lira had gone up to the long eave again, and this time she was away rather longer, and she came back walking fast.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:289:"It's one line," said Brom. "We've been here five days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:299:"Aren't you coming to look?" said Lira. "You read everything. You read the back of a waybill this morning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:309:He wrote the day's entry short. *Fourteenth day. First line: six crossings stood, nothing lost. Earned, two. In hand, less than one, and a side of bacon. The reserve is not counted here and never will be. Thirteenth of thirteen, Lira says. Deployment since the Line: none. Pressure, Storm and Wind all offered when a tree came down the river, and Brom's Path offered too, and all four were told no.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:311:"Two marks," said Lira, to the fire. "For standing still."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:319:"Then you had better show the rest of us how it's done," said Lira. "Because when that tree came round the bend I had one foot on the rail, and I don't remember putting it there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:3:He began with Lira, because she had asked first, and because she was a good deal the worst of them at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:21:The second time she pulled up on the line itself, stumbling, with her weight all wrong and her face dark with temper, and said that it was like running hard at a door and then leaving it shut. The third time she ran as she must have run on a painted floor with a coach watching. She came down the bar like something thrown; and three strides short of the line the air cracked, and the gravel went up in a fan, and she was standing twelve feet beyond his mark with her hands out in front of her and the heron lifting off the far bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:25:"I didn't do that," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:31:"That's what I wanted you to see. I couldn't have told you." He walked down to her. "Out here a burst is a shout. I don't know who it is that listens, and I don't think Brom's deer were telling us, but every carter on that road believes somebody does. And yours has just shouted without asking you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:45:Brom had found his own lesson without help. A pale trunk lay stranded at the water's edge, as thick as a man's body, and he got it up on his shoulder and began to carry it along the wet stones where the footing was worst. The rule was a simple one: when the load shifted, he was to let it fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:47:It shifted on the fourth step. Brom's whole arm went grey to the shoulder between one heartbeat and the next, and the trunk stayed where it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:51:"I know what it is." Brom set the trunk down slowly and stood looking at his arm until the colour came back. "It's never been part of the way before. It's all, or it's none. And I don't drop things."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:55:He took it up again. It rolled on his shoulder at the sixth step, and Cael saw him take a decision that plainly went against the grain of his whole life, and open his hands. The trunk hit the stones and bounced, and Brom stood over it, empty-handed, with the face of a man who has let fall somebody else's child.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:63:Karis sat on the bank above them with a river stone in her lap and one finger resting on it. She had explained it to him in three sentences, as she explained anything. To set a point was not to light it; she chose the place and held it ready, like a thumb on a latch; and the task was to hold it so. She counted under her breath. At ninety a flame no bigger than a seed stood up on the wet stone, and she took her finger away and looked at it with dislike.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:73:Seln was not on the bar. He was never anywhere at first light now, and came back when the meadow was stirring, with dust to his ankles and nothing to say about it; and Cael, who had spent two years being watched by that man, had decided to let him have his mornings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:75:Down the gravel Lira had turned and set her feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:81:He wrote it on the eighteenth day, at noon, on the bank above the bar, while Brom carried his tree and Lira ran. It took him the better part of an hour. He had written the nine out often enough, on a hill, as a list of what each could do. He had never set them down as what each one charged him to be carried and left alone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:85:*Wind, from Lira. It wants the first stride of anything. My feet lean before I've decided, a dozen times a day, and I put them back. If I ever spend a burst, the landing locks for one count, and that count has never got shorter. Rent: small, and constant.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:89:*Iron, from Brom: the read. A pace and a half round me, the mass of a thing and which way it is going, never what it means to do. It is the cheapest and quietest thing I own, and the hardest to leave shut, because it doesn't feel like doing anything. It feels like looking. I hold it anyway. I don't know what looking sounds like out here.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:93:*Ember, from Karis. One point, one channel. It wants to set a point on anything I look at for long. Karis lasted ninety today and I'd not have done better.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:95:*Shadow. Source protected; sealed, as it was on the hill, and for the hill's reason, which was who might carry the seeing west. It costs from the moment I wake and it drifts at dusk. It got as far as my boots on the fifth day. There are two reasons on it now where there was one: the seal, and whatever it is a road hears.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:99:*Anchor. Unnamed; the hand with no face. Fixed points, and lines between them, and a cage. I have unbound one thing on purpose, once, and I once held a doorway for twelve seconds without meaning to. It sees what is fixed: sure at ten feet, faint at twelve, nothing at fourteen. I don't know its reach, or its rate, or its price. Rent: ever since the Ostrand road it has asked about every sill and gate and doorway I've gone through, and a town with no wall has a great many doors.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:101:*Blade, from Ephram. Partial still. It asks "Edge?" of anything with a corner on it, takes a name, and settles. It was right once, on a scree.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:103:*Nine. And one anomaly, not counted, that came from nobody. Still open. Still real. Patience.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:109:"You've written *rent* seven times," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:111:She had come up the bank with her hair stuck to her forehead, and she was reading over his arm as she had read over it since Ardenmere. Brom had the same leave. Karis, who sat ten feet away with her stone, saw what he showed her and never once looked at the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:113:"Six. Compression doesn't charge, and Blade only asks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:129:Lira thought about that with her boot in her hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:137:They walked without talking, and by the middle of the morning Cael found that he had stopped missing it. Lira's long knife and Brom's bill rode where a stranger would see them, and at noon they sat on a bank and ate cold cake, and not one of the five so much as glanced at the dead wood lying under the trees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:143:It stayed with Brom, who cut for six hours that day, in a stand of straight grey poles above the river, with the old man's axe and the hedging bill, and whenever he stopped to wipe his face the white dog walked over and sat down on his feet. He told it twice that it was in the way, and after that he worked round it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:145:Cael watched him for a part of the first hour, while he and Lira were getting the measure of a spade and an iron bar. The axe was an old one, and its edge had been ground a little out of true, so that every tenth stroke or so it glanced in the grain and kicked back up the helve into the hands that held it. That was a load shifting, with no warning at all, and each time it happened Cael looked at Brom's wrists, and each time they were as brown as the rest of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:147:"You're counting," said Brom, not turning round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:153:He kept the count of his posts on a length of cord, a knot to each, hung over a branch. In the middle of the afternoon a goat came up out of the river meadow and ate the cord to the last knot but three, and Brom, who had the number perfectly well in his head, laid down the axe and went along the stack and counted every post over again with his finger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:157:Lira dug. She detested it, and said so at every hole, and was better at it than Cael by a long way, since she had a fighter's sense of where to put her weight. By evening they had eighteen posts in and the old fence drawn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:159:It was the new butts that Karis was looking at. She had read about fences, as she had read about most things, and she told Cael the principle while she chose a post from the stack. The end of a post that goes into the ground is charred first, to the depth of a finger, because rot will not take hold in charcoal; a fencer does it in a fire, and turns the post as he would turn a spit. She laid her post across two stones with its butt clear of the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:175:Karis had not moved. Her hand was still out, over the wood, and her mouth had come a little open; and Cael, who had sat beside her through fifty-one minutes in front of a hooded seat, watched her want something more than she had wanted to win that day. He saw her mouth close, and her hand come back into her lap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:187:"I think he would. I should have had a whole sentence from a primary source, and it's the one sentence I want in the world." Her voice was quite steady, and her knuckles were white on the pencil. "But the courier asked us nothing, and Pike asked you your age and stopped, and nobody in that meadow has yet asked me what's in my boxes. I believe that asking is a thing paper does. I should like to find out what one learns the other way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:191:"So far," said Karis, "one fact about a fence and one about myself. I'm not at all sure which I'd sooner have had."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:199:He was a long while about it, for he put his palm against every post of the forty and leaned, and the dog went with him and sat down each time he stopped. At the end he stood with his back to them and looked along the tops, where Brom had sighted each one against the last with a length of string. Then he went up to the house.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:201:He came back with four coins in a sock and a boy behind him carrying a sack on his shoulder. The coins he counted into Brom's hand. The sack, which was flour, and a good twenty pounds of it, the boy let down at Brom's feet as though that were the natural place for anything heavy. The old man scratched his mark on the slip with a nail, three upright strokes and a bar across them, and gave it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:209:"He means us," said Lira, on the road. She had gone a hundred yards with her jaw set. "*Cheap.* I dug twenty-two holes in that man's ground."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:211:"He means the work came to more than he paid for it," said Brom. He had the sack on one shoulder and the bill in his other hand, and he was walking more lightly than a man should with twenty pounds of flour. "He was pleased. He'd no other word for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:215:"It may be. I'll take it, though." Brom walked a little way. "I've been told what I was worth by a district panel and a provincial counter and a letter with a red seal on it. That's the first time anybody ever said it and thought he'd had the better of the bargain."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:227:"I heard it." Seln was a little ahead, on the verge, and his voice was hardly more than breath. "I was waiting to learn whether you would tell me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:237:Brom made bread that night, with the old man's flour, on a flat stone at the edge of their fire. He had seen it done, he said, and it could not be hard. The cakes came off the stone black on both faces and white in the middle, and Lira weighed one in her hand and asked whether the smith had been consulted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:239:"It's what the board paid," said Brom, with dignity.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:243:*Twenty-fourth day. Second line: forty posts, cut and set. Four marks and flour. He said "Cheap," and Brom has kept the word. Earned, six. At dusk the sealed one took my left foot for a stride, on an empty road. Rent. Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:247:Seln took the cold road on the thirtieth morning, and that was the last of the five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:255:It had not gone. He walked, and at each turn of the track he did the three things that he had always done. He found the place where a man would stand who wished to watch the road without being seen from it. He found the place where the road was narrowest. He looked for water, and paced it from where a camp might lie.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:263:It was long, and it stood straight, and he had found the same at every one of them, on five roads, for twenty days. A man who stands in a place, morning after morning, to watch a road leaves the mark of his feet there, and of his waiting, and Seln knew that mark as a baker knows his own thumb in a loaf. He had left it himself in every town he was ever posted to. There was none of it here. The grass had never been stood in, and he did not think that there was, in all this country, one person whose work it was to watch another and write down what he did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:273:He did not offer it. That was not sullenness; he had examined himself for it, with some care. He told them whatever bore on the day in hand. He had told them the gauge of the east road when a horse depended on it, and he had put five people where they ought to stand on a boat. The rest he kept, as he had always kept the rest, and he went about with it and waited for the level voice. *What did you see?* It would come at a fire, he supposed. The boy would say it, since the boy wrote everything down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:275:The boy had not said it; on the first night in the meadow he had looked at the dust on a pair of boots, and then he had very deliberately looked somewhere else. Seln had been watched for most of his life and had watched for the remainder. He could not remember being left a thing to keep before, and he had not yet settled how such a courtesy ought to be entered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:277:The case knocked against his hip. He turned at the crest, where the wind was, and went down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:289:"Before what water?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:293:They were at the wash in three hours, walking fast and saying nothing, with a coil of the ferryman's rope across Brom's chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:295:It was a dry stream cut deep through red earth, twelve feet down and thirty across at the bottom, with banks too steep to stand on. The road went into it by a ramp dug out of the eastern side and climbed out by another on the west. A trickle ran in the bed, no wider than a hand. Karis looked at that trickle before she looked at anything else, and went down and drove a peeled stick into the gravel at its edge, and cut a notch in the stick at the waterline with her knife.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:299:The woman with the hides was on the western lip with her two mules. She had got a third of her load up the ramp by herself, a bale at a time, and she was grey to the hair with dust. Cael knew her, for she had once laid a barley cake on the shaft of their cart, and she had been standing ten feet away when an old man asked him what he was. She pointed at the bed of the wash with her whole hand, and that was her greeting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:301:"Hides first," said Brom, very low. "Then it's a third the weight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:303:They made a chain of it and had the bales out in half an hour, and while the last of them went up, Brom walked round the wagon as he walked round any load. When he came back he had the whole of it, and he gave it to them with his hands as much as his voice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:305:A pole went under the broken corner, lashed fore and aft, so that the wagon would ride on three wheels and a skid. The long rope went from the tongue up the western ramp to the mules, and to Brom. That was the haul. But the bed fell away downstream, and a thing dragged across a slope will always swing its tail to the low side and go over; so a second line must run from the tail-ring back to the eastern bank, and somebody must stand there and pay it out, a foot at a time, and never let it go slack.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:307:"That's the light end," he said, and looked at Lira. "It wants somebody quick. There's a thorn stump on the lip. Two turns round it, and the stump holds the wagon. You only hold the stump."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:309:Lira walked down the bed and up the far ramp with the line over her shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:311:Cael had the wedge. It was a block of ash, split from a limb beside a crossroads fire, and he had carried it over a line and down a scree, and stopped a cart with it some hundreds of times. Seln had a pole to lever with, and they took a wheel each.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:313:"One notch," said Karis, from the gravel. "The water's brown."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:315:The mules leaned, and the wagon groaned and came a yard, grinding, and the skid bit and held, and Cael kicked the wedge in under the hind wheel. It came another yard, and on the eastern lip Lira let a foot of line go round the stump, and then another, with her whole body bent back against it, and the tail came round sweetly behind the nose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:319:The wagon lurched over toward its broken corner, and for the space of a breath it hung there, deciding. The check line came up out of the dust as taut as a bowstring. Lira went forward two strides on her heels, toward the edge, and Cael saw her knees bend in the way he had seen on the gravel bar, three strides short of a line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:325:Brom's boots went from under him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:327:Cael saw the grey start at both wrists, and he saw it stop. Then Brom did the only thing that a very large man can do who has decided to be nothing more than that: he sat down. He sat down hard in the dirt of the ramp with the rope round his body and his heels dug in, and was dragged a yard on the seat of his breeches, and another foot, cutting two furrows; and there, like an anchor that has found bottom, he held.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:331:*Wheel*, he told it. *Hers. Leave it.* He drove the ash under the iron with his heel, and Seln's pole came down beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:333:"I've sat down," said Brom, through his teeth, from the ramp. "It's all I had."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:335:"Three notches," said Karis. "And there's a sound."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:337:It came from up the wash, low and steady, like a cart crossing a wooden bridge a long way off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:339:The check line had fouled. Cael saw it as the mule found its feet again: a loop of it had dropped off the lip and caught behind a rock in the bed, twenty feet out from the eastern bank, and while it lay there Lira could neither pay out nor take in. She had seen it too, from above. It was twenty feet of open air to that rock, and she could have been standing on it in the time it takes to blink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:343:"Now," said Brom, and the mules leaned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:345:The wagon went up the western ramp on three wheels and a pole, a yard and a wedge at a time. Its tailboard had cleared the lip by perhaps the length of the wagon when the wash filled, and the water did not come as a wave. It came as a brown tongue a foot high, quite quiet, with sticks in it, and behind the tongue the bed simply rose. Karis came up the ramp at a run with her skirts in her fists, and when Cael looked back from the top her notched stick was gone, and the place where a wagon had lain was hip deep and moving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:347:The woman with the hides scratched her mark on the slip against her own tailboard. She had not spoken a word in three hours. She looked now at Brom, who was red earth from his heels to his belt, and at Lira's hands; and last she looked at Cael, for a good while, with her head a little on one side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:357:Lira came and stood by him while the hides went on again. Her palms were raw across the heel, and she was holding them a little away from her coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:3:They walked behind the three carts for two days more, and in all that time nobody spoke to them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:5:It was not unfriendly, for Cael had been ignored by experts, in a house on a bluff, and he knew the feel of it; this was something else, and he came to think of it as being let alone. On the eighth evening the woman with the hides walked back along the road at dusk and laid a flat cake on the shaft of their cart, still warm from being carried inside her coat, and walked away again before anybody could thank her. Lira broke it in five, and Brom said it was barley, and nobody found anything to add.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:7:The country came down as they went. The red trees thinned and gave over to a broad pale tree with leaves that showed silver when the wind turned them, which Karis also declined to name, and the river they were following grew slow and wide and took in others. It was warm in the bottom. The dust stood behind the carts until long after they had passed, and there were flies about the gelding's eyes all day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:13:It lay on the far side of a river a hundred yards across, green-brown and unhurried, and he read it from the water upward, as he had been taught to read a floor. There was a landing, with a flat boat at it. Above the landing stood a long low building with a deep eave, and on the rise behind that a taller one with two chimneys; between them a smithy was sending up the first smoke he had seen in three days. Along the river below the buildings ran a meadow, half a mile of it, with a single great ash standing in the middle and carts and tents scattered round the tree like washing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:21:"There is no anything," said Seln. He had come up on the other side of the cart, and he was not looking at the buildings at all. He was looking at what left them: a road that went north along the water, and another that climbed east over a saddle, and a third going south into low hills. "I count four ways out, with ours, and I think there is a fifth that somebody does not much use. I should like to know where each of them goes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:23:"Ask someone," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:31:"That's a mule," said Brom. He had his hand over his eyes. "An old one. He's got a bad off hind, and he's being asked to do it anyway."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:37:The carts went first, as was plainly their right, two to a crossing. A boy took a copper for each wheel and a copper for each beast, and he looked at the gelding as the courier had looked at it, and charged for it as a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:39:Their own turn came last. The gelding had not cared for the Ost and did not care for this, and he went onto the planks sideways, with the whites of his eyes showing. Lira told him in a hoarse steady voice exactly what she thought of an animal that had forded three rivers on his own feet and now objected to being carried over a fourth. Halfway across the rope went slack. They hung there on the cable, turning a little, with the water sliding by underneath, until the tired voice on the bank had said its say and the drum began to creak again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:43:Nobody told them where to stand in the meadow, so they stood and watched where everyone else stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:45:The carts lay along the river side with their shafts down and a stone behind each wheel. The tents were farther in, in no order he could find, of hide and tarred canvas and old sail, and they were not the same tents from one hour to the next. While Brom was getting the gelding out of the shafts a crew of four struck one within thirty yards of him, rolled it, and walked out by the north road with it on a pole between two of them; and before the flattened grass had lifted, a woman with a handcart had pitched on the same place. He did not see anybody ask anybody's leave. He had counted seventeen tents from the ferry, out of habit, and there were twenty when he counted again; and by evening only nine of the first seventeen stood where they had stood at noon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:49:"That's his," said Lira. "The tree. Look how they've left it for him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:51:"Or he is only the first who thought of it, and the least pleasant to move," said Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:57:There were fires here, and he saw it almost at once, and felt Karis see it beside him, for her pencil came out of the spine of her notebook. There were cook-fires at half the tents, and a woman frying something over one, and smoke lying along the river in a low blue shelf. People were talking, too, in ordinary voices, and somebody laughed aloud. Down by the smithy a man was holding the end of an iron bar across the anvil with his bare forearm, and the arm was grey to the elbow, and nobody round him so much as turned to look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:59:"So it is the road," said Karis, very low, writing. "It isn't a rule about fire, or about speaking, or about a Path. It's a rule about doing any of them on a road. Here they do all three." She underlined something. "That's narrower than I had it. I don't know that it's any clearer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:61:"Does it change the ruling?" said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:65:A boy came for the standing-money. He was the same sort of boy as the one at the ferry, brown and quick, with a tally stick in his belt, and he walked round the cart and counted its wheels aloud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:67:"A copper a night to stand. The grass is free for a mule." He looked at the gelding, and then up at Brom, with some sympathy. "He'll not do on the grass, will he. They don't. Hay's by the net."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:71:The boy told him, and told him as well how much a horse of that size would want of them in a day, which he seemed to know from sad experience of other people's horses. Brom's face did not move at all, but he stood on with his hand on the gelding's neck when there was nothing further to ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:73:"And if we want work?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:77:Lira was already walking. Cael knew that walk, having followed it down a lane in Ardenmere toward a yard with a groove in it, and into every hall since; it was how she went toward anything that might have her name on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:81:He went after her. Behind him Karis had lifted a corner of the canvas and was counting to three, and Brom was doing some arithmetic of his own, with his lips; and when Cael looked for the fifth of them, he could find no dark coat anywhere in the meadow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:89:Lira went along it fast, with her finger out, and her face fell as she went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:113:There were eight more. One wanted a night-watch kept on the landing, and one wanted stones picked out of a field, and one was a message to be walked to a hold four days off, for a mark and feed. Most were in the same square black hand, as if one person had written them out for others; but beside the words on each somebody had drawn a little picture, a wheel or a fence or a boat with sticks standing in it, for the use of people who could make nothing of the words.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:115:"Eleven bales against one crew," said Lira, who had come back along the plank and was reading the fleece over his shoulder. "I'd back the bales. Is that all there is? Carrying wool down a hill?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:123:"That's worse," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:127:Every name on the board in Ardenmere had carried letters after it, and a number: *Cu 3*, and the like. They had been ratings, borrowed from the registry's tiers and kept in somebody's private ledger, and a stranger could not have stood in that yard for a minute without being told where he stood among them. He had learned to read a card by them before he learned anything else. Here there was a job, and whoever wanted it done, and what they would pay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:129:Not one of the fourteen said who might take it. None of them asked for a tier, or a rank, or a Path of any kind, and none said *Iron or above*, or *a Bronze to lead*. The slip with the seal on it wanted a crew to stand on a boat, and it was all one to that slip whether the crew were five Golds or five carters' boys.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:133:Lira looked along the plank again, more slowly. He watched her understand it, and it was not a happy understanding. She had gone up a ladder a rung at a time since she was a girl, at Fenmark and in a yard and on a hill, and had paid for every rung with her feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:149:He looked at Lira and then at Cael. Then he looked at each of them again, in the same order, just as carefully, as though the first look had been only a draft.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:153:"Are you the keeper?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:165:The others had come up while he spoke, Brom large and quiet, and Karis with her notebook shut on her finger. At the plank's far end, reading the last slip as if he had been there for some time, stood a mild man in a dark coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:167:"Copper," said Brom slowly. "And you keep the book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:171:"We want work," said Lira. "There's no bouts. What do crews do, here?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:183:Nobody answered him, for Lira looked at Cael, and Cael looked at Brom, and Brom looked at the ground, and Karis opened her notebook as though it might be written there. It had never once come up. They had been four enrollees and a clerk; before that they had been a provision, and a ladder fighter, and a docket, and a research budget, and a man who sent in returns.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:189:"We put our hands up," said Brom. "All five. It's worked so far."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:195:They said them, Lira as she would have given hers to a registrar, and Brom as he would have told the hour, and Karis spelled hers out letter by letter. Then there was a gap, no longer than a breath, and Cael knew whose it was without turning his head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:199:"Seln," said Seln. He seemed a little surprised by the sound of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:233:Seln came back at dusk and did not say where he had been. He sat down in his own place, a little behind the rest, and held his hands out to the flames, and there was dust on his boots to the ankle that was not the meadow's dust.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:235:"He never asked," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:241:"I'd mine ready as well," said Brom. "Copper. For good, and I can say why." He turned his big hands over. "And then the man with the book's a Copper himself, and he says it the way I'd say I was tall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:243:"Iron, Rank Three," said Karis. "Certified, and retained. I've not had it counted since a counter, and I find that I expected him to count it." She considered. "I think I was looking forward to his declining."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:245:"Bronze," said Seln mildly. "Nobody has asked me in fifteen years. They had it on a roll."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:251:Nobody laughed, and Brom nodded slowly, as a man nods at a weight he has also lifted, and Karis wrote something short and did not underline it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:255:"I don't see how it works," said Lira. "That's what I can't get past. If there's no rank, then how does anybody out here know I'm better than I was last month?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:257:"You go out," said Brom. "And you come back."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:265:"There's a sheet on the wall by the hearth. I didn't go in. Twelve lines on it, one under another." She put her chin back on her knees. "I couldn't read them from the door, and there was a room full of people who'd have watched me try. I'll read it when I'm on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:267:Karis had the field archive open, by the light. She had written five words across the head of a clean leaf, in her small exact hand, with a ruled line under them. Now she went back along the row and drew a second line beneath one of the five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:269:"You've underlined it," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:275:"Because the other four are facts about a contract. Who wanted what done, and who saw it, and what was paid." Karis laid the pencil in the fold. "That one is a fact about us. It's the only place in his whole book where he writes down anything we did. I should like to know what words he uses."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:283:He shut the book and looked across the fire, to where the gelding had his nose in a net by the cart. Brom had gone over to stand with him, and was counting something on his fingers, slowly, and then he counted it a second time, and from the set of his shoulders he liked the answer no better.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:7:The fire had burned down to a red eye, and Cael let it go, for Brom had said at the start of the watch that a man who feeds a fire all night ends by looking at the fire, and Brom was generally right about anything that had weight. So he sat with his back to the embers and his face to the dark, on a shelf of turf that nobody had ever troubled to name, and he counted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:9:It was an old habit, and he had it from a hill. For a year there had been two men at the foot of a road with a tin cup between them, and a third on a tier with a pencil, and a mild clerk at a copying table who had turned out to be the best of the four; and he had learned to find each of them in the first minute of any morning, as a man feels for his purse. He ran the count now over a country he could not see. He took the ridge behind them first, and the scree, and the black shoulder to the north where a night bird kept its two notes, and then the gulf in front, in sections, from the near ground outward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:15:"Zero," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:21:"I can hear you stop breathing at the end of each one." Brom spoke low, as he had been taught in an orchard. "That's three. It's zero. I've checked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:27:Cael let his breath go, and found that Brom had been right about that as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:33:Shadow, the quiet one, had lifted at dusk, as it lifted at every dusk, soft behind all the rest, and he had held it where it was while Seln laid a fire ten feet away. Blade was the newest of the nine and the quickest, and it had asked about the edge of the scree, and for once had been told that it was right. Storm did nothing whatever. It was a Gold's worth of weather, the heaviest fragment he owned, and it sat in him as a full pail sits on a step, so that he was aware of it all day in the way that one is aware of a pail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:37:He took Hesk's clock out of its stocking and tilted the face to the embers, and it said a quarter past the hour. It lost, as it always had, and a researcher with her pencil out had ruled two days ago that it was right by definition, being the only clock east of a line. He supposed the watch was over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:39:Lira woke as she did everything. He had put two fingers on her shoulder, and she was sitting up with her hand shut on his wrist before he had taken them away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:49:"Do what Brom does, and count us." She was feeling about for her boots. "We're harder. We move."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:51:Karis woke more slowly. She put out one pale hand before her eyes were open, and it found the canvas and went along it to the knot over the middle box, and rested there. Then she sat up and asked what the clock said, and wrote the figure down before she had so much as looked at the sky.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:55:Seln lay under the pine, on his side, with an empty satchel beneath his head. He had been out-argued at the fire by his own syllabus, and had lain down to prove a table right, and somewhere in the first hour he had actually gone to sleep. Cael had sat across a counter from that face for two years and had never once seen it with nothing on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:57:"Leave him," said Lira, very low. "He's the fifth. It's in the table."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:69:There was as much of it under the shelf as Brom had said, and it was no kinder than what lay above. The boxes went down a leg at a time, and the cart followed on its locked wheels with a large man leaning back on a rope; and Cael went ahead with the wedge, his back to the slope, and said where the edge was. He said it forty times that morning, and Blade agreed with every one of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:73:It had looked like a thread from the ridge. It was a river, forty feet of fast brown water over stones, the colour of weak tea, and so loud after the silence of the hill that Lira had to say everything twice. Trees stood along it, which he had seen from above as a darkness; and they were a darkness still, tall and close, with bark as red as a fox and a smell he could put no name to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:75:"Pine," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:77:"It isn't a pine." Karis had a twig of it and was turning it over. "The needles are flat, and they come in pairs. I don't know what it is, and I shan't write a name for a thing I can't source."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:83:"Red not-pine, then. You'll want to call it something when you're telling Brom to tie the horse to it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:85:Karis looked at the twig. Then she wrote, and Cael, who was near enough, read it over her arm. *Conifer, red-barked, needles flat and paired; unidentified. L. says "red not-pine." Entered as L.'s.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:87:"That's my name in a book," said Lira. "For a tree."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:91:The track was there, on the near bank. It was only two ruts with a ridge of grass between them, as it had been on the upland. But it kept to the river and it ran downstream, and that was more than any of them had been promised. Lira opened her own book on her forearm and wrote as she walked, while Karis kept the printed sheet rolled under her arm and did not once unroll it, since there was nothing on that part of it but a list of what the symbols meant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:93:Brom walked at the tailboard and looked at the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:95:He had done so all the way from Ostrand, and Cael had taken it for tiredness, and been wrong. Brom was reading, slowly, as he read anything, and a little after noon he gave them what he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:97:"Mules," he said. "Small feet, and narrow. A good many, going both ways, and none of them lately." He nodded at the gelding's quarters. "And one horse, which is him. There's not a shoe-print on this road that isn't his."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:99:"Is that bad?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:103:They made eleven miles by Karis's count of the gelding's stops, which she kept in a column, and toward evening they came on a place where people had halted before them. It lay a little back from the water, on a flat, where the grass was worn to earth and there was old dung, small and dry. In the middle of the bare ground somebody had set a ring of stones. Cael was glad of that ring out of all proportion to what it was, for it was the first thing since a station's sill that a person had made on purpose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:105:Karis paced it from the bank and got forty-one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:109:"Or it is only the nearest flat," said Seln, and went on walking the edges of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:111:Brom had the gelding out of the shafts. He stood over the ring a while with the hobbles in his hand; then he crouched, and turned one of the stones over with two fingers, and turned it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:115:"It's rained," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:121:Brom thought about it honestly. He had no answer, and he said so, and went to see to the horse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:123:So they laid their own fire in it. Seln's flint was at the bottom of his coat. Karis put out one finger and lit the kindling with a point no bigger than a pin's head, as she had lit a page for Cael under a cart two nights before. The red wood caught fast and burned with a high clear flame; and its smoke stood up out of the valley, straight, in the still air, to twice the height of the trees, where anything with eyes on any of seven ridges might have looked at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:133:"Straight over," said Lira, who had the gelding's cheek-strap. "It's nothing. It's the upland one again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:135:"Then why has somebody built two cairns?" said Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:141:Lira looked, and she had her mouth open to answer when a mule came down through the red trees on the far side, with a rider on it, and walked into the river at the cairn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:143:The rider did not hail them. She put the mule's nose at the upstream cairn on their own bank and came across on the slant, a long quartering line, and the water was never over the animal's knee. Halfway she passed within ten yards of the place where Lira had meant to cross, and Cael watched the surface there as she went by: it was smooth, and darker than the rest, and it turned slowly in its own length, like something asleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:145:"Oh," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:147:"How deep?" said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:149:"I don't know. Over the bed of the cart." She let go of the strap and wiped her hand down her coat. "I'd have put three boxes in that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:153:She looked them over. She took in the horse's feet, and the iron on the wheels, and the shape of the canvas, and gave each about as long as a clerk gives a line he has seen before. Then she lifted her chin at the cairn beside her and at the one across the water, once each, and turned the mule, and rode it forty yards down the bank before she let it drink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:157:"She is going west," said Seln quietly. "I think she will be the only one who is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:159:Cael had the two letters inside his coat. He had carried them two days in the pocket of Hesk's book, where a Gold's letter had lain for most of a year, and he had not expected to be rid of them before a town. He walked down the bank to her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:175:He waited. A ford-keeper had asked him for his name not a fortnight ago, and had been a little sorry not to get it, and a man on a rock had wanted to know where she was to write to. So he stood on the shingle with two coppers in his hand, and waited to be asked what the letters were, and who he was, and where five people thought they were going with a shod horse and three roped boxes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:185:"Is that wrong?" said Lira, from the cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:193:They took the cart over on the slanting line, cairn to cairn, and the water never reached the hub. On the far bank Lira walked the gelding forty yards downstream before she let him put his head down, and she did it without a word to anybody, with her chin up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:197:*Fourth day. The letters to Hesk and to Vell went west by a courier on a mule, a copper each, as far as the turf fire. She showed us the ford by riding it. She did not ask what the letters were. She did not ask what we were either. Entered, because I can't remember the last person who didn't.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:207:Other ruts came into theirs out of side valleys, one after another, like streams joining; and by the fifth day's noon the track was a cart wide and beaten hard, with the grass gone from its middle. The valley had opened. The red trees stood back on the slopes, and there was pale grass in the bottom, shoulder high, that went over in the wind like a cat under a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:209:There were deer in it, of a sort. They were grey and long in the leg, with white rumps, and they stood in the shallows by threes and watched the cart go by with the steady disapproval of magistrates. Brom watched them back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:213:"Should they be?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:221:The gelding's shoes rang on the stones. It had not mattered on the upland, where there was turf, and Cael had not heard it in the ruts. But the road was hard now, and wherever rock came through it the iron struck, and the valley gave the sound back. He caught Seln listening to it, with his head a little on one side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:223:"He can be heard from that ridge," said Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:225:"He's a horse," said Lira. "He's got feet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:227:"So has a mule, and a mule goes unshod on this ground. I have been looking at the prints for two days, as the large one has, and I do not think that anybody on this road has ever wished to be heard from a ridge." He considered the gelding without ill will. "I do not say he is wrong. I say he is an announcement."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:233:Lira opened her mouth, and shut it, and walked on with her hand a little tighter on the strap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:241:It was Shadow, the one fragment of the nine that he kept sealed. He knew it at once, and he was ready, as he had been ready at every dusk since a door log in a wool exchange. It came up soft behind the other eight and offered him what it always offered, which was to make less of him. He told it no. He had told it no so many times that the word had worn smooth, and he was tired, with eleven miles in his legs, and he said it as a man says good night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:249:"Your left boot went first," said Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:255:"You have it now. For four strides you had not, and by the second it was both feet." Seln walked on a little. When he spoke again it was no louder, and the others, at the horse's head, could not have heard. "I should say that I know it. I learned it on a stair, as a boy, by wanting very much not to be heard by the person at the top. It took me a year to do on purpose and three more to stop doing by accident. It always came at dusk, when the eye is worst and the ear must do the work."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:265:Seln went forward to the wheel and laid a hand on the tailboard, as though it had needed steadying, which it had not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:269:*Fifth day. Deployment since the Line: none. At dusk, on an empty road, Shadow got as far as my boots, seal and all. Four strides, both feet, before I had it shut. I didn't ask for it and I didn't use it for anything, so it isn't a deployment. It's what the thing costs to carry. Call it rent. Entered as rent, and Seln heard it, which is right. He would.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:273:They came up with the carts in the middle of the sixth morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:275:There were three, going the same way, at a mule's walk. They were narrow things, high in the wheel, hardly more than half the width of the road, and Cael saw why when the first of them went by a place where the bank had slipped: it passed with a foot to spare, and their own cart, coming after, had to be lifted round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:277:"Don't pass," said Lira. She had sat on a landing wall for a week learning carters, and she said it as a rule. "You don't go by a cart on a road. You fall in behind, and you wait until somebody speaks to you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:283:So they fell in, forty yards back. A woman walked beside the last cart, which was stacked with hides under a net, and she looked round once, at the gelding's feet, with a look that Cael had begun to know. Then she faced front again, and that was all the greeting there was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:285:He watched them for the whole of a day, having nothing else to do with his eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:289:They wore steel, and they wore it outside. The old man had a hatchet through his belt, over his coat, with the edge forward, and the woman carried a long knife on her thigh and had a boar spear slung in rings along the cart's side, where one pull would free it; even the boy had a hedging bill, too big for him. None of it was hidden and none of it was held. It was simply there to be seen, like the courier's hatchet in its loop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:291:At noon they stopped and ate, and Lira went a few steps up the bank, by habit, for sticks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:293:"Don't," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:299:The carters sat on the shafts of their own carts, each with a cloth open on the knee, eating something cold and flat, and none of them had so much as glanced at the wood lying under the trees. Lira came back down the bank with her hands empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:301:They halted at dusk, where the road widened by the river. The old man pulled his cart off the crown and the boy pulled in behind him. Then each of them walked to a ring of stones in the trodden ground, and took up one in either hand, and set it behind a wheel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:303:"Chocks," said Brom, under his breath, like a man who has found his own boot. "It's where they keep the chocks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:307:The boy came back along the carts while there was still some light. He had been looking at the gelding over his shoulder all day and wanted a nearer look. He grinned at Lira as boys had been grinning at her since Ardenmere, and stood there with his thumbs in his belt. Then he seemed to feel that five strangers and a horse deserved something of him. He took out his right hand and turned it palm up, with the fingers spread, as Karis spread hers before she set a point.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:311:It was not a blow. The old man had come up behind him without a sound, and he put the boy's arm down as one puts down the lid of a box, and the boy's ears went red.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:315:He did not raise his voice, and in that quiet he had no need to. The boy went away along the carts with his head down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:317:The old man stayed where he was. He looked at the iron tyres, and the three shapes under the canvas, and the shod horse, and at each of the five of them in turn, and he was a good while about it. He had pale eyes, and the skin round them was folded very small.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:329:Cael sat down on the cart's shaft. He did what he had done on the east tier of a yard every week for two years, with a notebook on his knee, and it steadied him as it always had: he set out the claim, and what stood under it, and then he ruled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:333:Under it he had five things. Three carts had gone a day without a word, and every one of their people wore an edge outside the coat, the boy included. There were rings of stones on the halting-grounds that had never been burned in, because they had never been meant for fire. A courier had counted their smokes from a ridge and told them it was their wood. And a boy had been about to show them something, and had had it taken out of his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:339:They were all near enough to hear. Lira had her chin on her fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:345:"I haven't done one thing since the Line," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:347:"Karis has lit three fires with her finger."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:349:"Four," said Karis. "There was the one under the cart. I've entered all of them, with the hour." She had gone a little paler. "I shall enter that I didn't know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:351:"And if a wheel goes down in a river?" said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:359:Brom thought that over, and seemed to find it sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:361:"And you?" said Lira, looking at him. "You've nine fragments, and not one of them's a Path. Does it count?"

