   300	
   301	"Not yet."
   302	
   303	"Why not?"
   304	
   305	He thought about how to say it, for he had been on a great many boards. A crossroads in Ardenmere had carried him, in chalk, and a hall at Norhold, and four black ones in a sunk yard, and every one of them had told the people looking at it what to think of him before he opened his mouth.
   306	
   307	"I'll read it when there's something on it that I did," he said. "One line for standing on a boat isn't worth the walk. I'd like the first thing that sheet tells me to be true."
   308	
   309	He wrote the day's entry short. *Fourteenth day. First line: six crossings stood, nothing lost. Earned, two. In hand, less than one, and a side of bacon. The reserve is not counted here and never will be. Thirteenth of thirteen, Lira says. Deployment since the Line: none. Pressure, Storm and Wind all offered when a tree came down the river, and Brom's Path offered too, and all four were told no.*
   310	
   311	"Two marks," said Lira, to the fire. "For standing still."
   312	
   313	"It's harder than it looks."
   314	
   315	"You've had practice."
   316	
   317	"Two years of it."
   318	
   319	"Then you had better show the rest of us how it's done," said Lira. "Because when that tree came round the bend I had one foot on the rail, and I don't remember putting it there."

