    14	
    15	"Yes."
    16	
    17	"Is it wrong?"
    18	
    19	"No."
    20	
    21	Pike nodded, and turned his shoulder to the meadow, and said it out over the heads of the people at the plank in his level, carrying voice, so that it went as far as the ash.
    22	
    23	"Thornwater road, the stretch. Walked ahead of one cart, one carter, one mule. Delivered at the fence. No losses. Nine marks, paid."
    24	
    25	It was strange to hear. Cael had read those words on a scrap of bark in a hold's yard, and thought them his; and now they belonged to a woman with a board of loaves on her head, and a man mending a trace, and an old carter under a tree, who had lifted his chin at the word *stretch* and held it there to the end. Several of the people at the plank turned and looked at the five of them. They looked once, at their boots and their steel, and then went back to their own affairs.
    26	
    27	Pike unlocked the iron box and counted nine coins onto the oak, in threes. Karis took them up, since the purse was hers to keep. She did not count them a second time in front of him, which Cael knew she would do later.
    28	
    29	A woman was next. She was lean and burnt, with a strip of red cloth tied round her hair, and two men and a girl stood at her back; she laid a slip on the plank and stepped away from it with her arms folded. Pike read it to himself, and read it again, and then he gave it to the meadow.
    30	
