    63	Karis sat on the bank above them with a river stone in her lap and one finger resting on it. She had explained it to him in three sentences, as she explained anything. To set a point was not to light it; she chose the place and held it ready, like a thumb on a latch; and the task was to hold it so. She counted under her breath. At ninety a flame no bigger than a seed stood up on the wet stone, and she took her finger away and looked at it with dislike.
    64	
    65	"Ninety," she said. "I've never kept one waiting half so long. I've never had cause. I set them without thinking, on a doorpost, or the corner of a page. I should think I've left a dozen standing behind me in every room I ever worked in." She wrote the figure down. "And I don't know whether a point that's set and not lit is a thing this country hears. Nobody has told us what the rule is a rule against."
    66	
    67	"Then you don't set one. Not on a road."
    68	
    69	"I haven't gone a day without one since I was fourteen."
    70	
    71	"I know," said Cael. "Those are the worst to stop. The ones you never knew you were doing."
    72	
    73	Seln was not on the bar. He was never anywhere at first light now, and came back when the meadow was stirring, with dust to his ankles and nothing to say about it; and Cael, who had spent two years being watched by that man, had decided to let him have his mornings.
    74	
    75	Down the gravel Lira had turned and set her feet.
