    78	
    79	He had told three people what it cost them, and he thought he owed the book the same account of himself.
    80	
    81	He wrote it on the eighteenth day, at noon, on the bank above the bar, while Brom carried his tree and Lira ran. It took him the better part of an hour. He had written the nine out often enough, on a hill, as a list of what each could do. He had never set them down as what each one charged him to be carried and left alone.
    82	
    83	*Eighteenth day. The nine, as things held. Not what they do. What it costs to have each one and not use it, which I'm calling rent.*
    84	
    85	*Wind, from Lira. It wants the first stride of anything. My feet lean before I've decided, a dozen times a day, and I put them back. If I ever spend a burst, the landing locks for one count, and that count has never got shorter. Rent: small, and constant.*
    86	
    87	*Pressure, from the Bronze from Ardenmere. It gathers over most of a breath and anybody can see it gathering. The easiest to refuse, because it is the slowest to arrive. It tried at the tree on the river.*
    88	
    89	*Iron, from Brom: the read. A pace and a half round me, the mass of a thing and which way it is going, never what it means to do. It is the cheapest and quietest thing I own, and the hardest to leave shut, because it doesn't feel like doing anything. It feels like looking. I hold it anyway. I don't know what looking sounds like out here.*
    90	
    91	*Compression, from Reydan. Only at contact; the load goes through me into the ground and the bill comes later. Nothing to hold until something hits me.*
    92	
    93	*Ember, from Karis. One point, one channel. It wants to set a point on anything I look at for long. Karis lasted ninety today and I'd not have done better.*
    94	
    95	*Shadow. Source protected; sealed, as it was on the hill, and for the hill's reason, which was who might carry the seeing west. It costs from the moment I wake and it drifts at dusk. It got as far as my boots on the fifth day. There are two reasons on it now where there was one: the seal, and whatever it is a road hears.*
    96	
    97	*Storm, from Daeva. Gold weight, and the heaviest thing I carry, and idle it does nothing at all but sit. If I open it, the read is the air or my own body and the ground, never both, and a lane takes the air. Three lanes so far. After each, everything I have answered a beat late: for ten counts, and ten, and twelve, and came clean at eleven, eleven and thirteen. That is a bill, not a flag. I have not tried a fourth. Rent while idle: I tire sooner than the miles account for.*
    98	
    99	*Anchor. Unnamed; the hand with no face. Fixed points, and lines between them, and a cage. I have unbound one thing on purpose, once, and I once held a doorway for twelve seconds without meaning to. It sees what is fixed: sure at ten feet, faint at twelve, nothing at fourteen. I don't know its reach, or its rate, or its price. Rent: ever since the Ostrand road it has asked about every sill and gate and doorway I've gone through, and a town with no wall has a great many doors.*
   100	
   101	*Blade, from Ephram. Partial still. It asks "Edge?" of anything with a corner on it, takes a name, and settles. It was right once, on a scree.*
   102	
   103	*Nine. And one anomaly, not counted, that came from nobody. Still open. Still real. Patience.*
   104	
   105	*Deployment since the Line: none.*
   106	
