editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:157:"The estate's paid me a son's allowance since I was fourteen," said Brom. "Every month, whether I answered the letter or I didn't. I never earned a copper of it, and I never went hungry, and I can't tell you what I'm worth to anybody, because nobody's ever had to pay it. The registry says Copper and a district panel says Iron, and neither of them has ever paid me either." He turned his big hands over and looked at the palms. "There's a man here with one arm who'll write down what I did and what it fetched. I'd like to read that line without knowing there's a bag behind it."
